OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026", "title": "Universal optimality of the triangular lattice", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "090", "family_title": "Triangular-lattice optimality, long-range Riesz and Coulomb energies, and spherical logarithmic energy", "abstract_html": "We prove universal energy minimality for the triangular lattice in the plane. Among locally finite configurations of centered density one, it minimizes the lower limit of centered-ball energy averages for every nonnegative completely monotone function of squared distance, including when the energy is infinite. We also prove triangular minimality for planar logarithmic and Riesz renormalized energies, with \\(0\\lt s\\lt 2\\) in the Riesz case, and the corresponding jellium minima. The proof uses sharp Gaussian Fourier bounds, positive mixtures, and heat-kernel comparison.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every locally finite \\(C\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) of centered disk density one, \\( E_g(C)\\ge\\sum_{a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}}g(|a|^2)=E_g(A) \\) in the extended nonnegative reals.", "statement": "<p>**Universal energy minimality — paper Theorem 1.1.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(b=\\sqrt3/2\\), and let\n  \\[\n  A=b^{-1/2}\\{j(1,0)+k(1/2,b):j,k\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\n  \\]\n  be the triangular lattice of covolume one.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B_R\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) be the closed disk of radius \\(R\\) centered at the origin. For a locally finite configuration \\(C\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\), put\n  \\[\n  C_R=C\\cap B_R,\\qquad N_R=\\#C_R.\n  \\]\n  Centered disk density one means\n  \\[\n  \\frac{N_R}{\\pi R^2}\\longrightarrow1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonnegative interaction \\(g:(0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)\\), define the ordered-pair energy\n  \\[\n  E_g(C)=\\liminf_{R\\to\\infty}\\frac1{N_R}\n  \\sum_{\\substack{x,y\\in C_R\\\\x\\ne y}}g(|x-y|^2).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that \\(g\\) is smooth and completely monotone:\n  \\[\n  (-1)^r g^{(r)}(t)\\ge0\n  \\qquad(r\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\ t&gt;0).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every locally finite \\(C\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) of centered disk density one,\n\\[\nE_g(C)\\ge\\sum_{a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}}g(|a|^2)=E_g(A)\n\\]\nin the extended nonnegative reals. No separation or local occupancy bound is imposed on \\(C\\). The statement includes singular potentials and divergent lattice sums and identifies the minimum value, not all minimizers.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 7 proves the Gaussian hypothesis of Proposition 8 for every locally finite configuration of centered disk density one. Proposition 8 therefore gives\n\\[\nE_g(C)\\ge\\sum_{a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}}g(|a|^2).\n\\]</p><p>For attainment, choose a bounded half-open fundamental cell of \\(A\\), with vertex zero and area one, and let \\(d\\) bound the distance from its points to its lattice vertex. Up to area-zero boundaries, for \\(R&gt;d\\), the union of cells with vertices in \\(A\\cap B_R\\) contains \\(B_{R-d}\\) and is contained in \\(B_{R+d}\\). Its area is \\(\\#(A\\cap B_R)\\), so \\(A\\) has centered disk density one.</p><p>For each \\(x\\in A\\cap B_R\\), the differences \\(y-x\\), with \\(y\\in A\\cap B_R\\setminus\\{x\\}\\), form a subset of \\(A\\setminus\\{0\\}\\). Nonnegativity gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{y\\in A\\cap B_R\\\\y\\ne x}}g(|x-y|^2)\n\\le\\sum_{a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}}g(|a|^2).\n\\]\nIf the lattice sum is finite, averaging supplies the upper bound on \\(E_g(A)\\), while the proved inequality applied to \\(A\\) gives the reverse bound. If the sum is infinite, that same lower bound gives \\(E_g(A)=+\\infty\\). Thus equality and inequality both hold in the extended nonnegative reals.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every positive integer \\(n\\), put \\(N=n^2\\) and enumerate \\(A/(nA)\\) by \\(a_1^\\triangle,\\ldots,a_N^\\triangle\\).", "statement": "<p>**Planar renormalized and jellium energies — paper Corollary 8.1.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be the unit-covolume triangular lattice defined in Theorem 1, and let \\(0\\le s&lt;2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  g_s(X)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  -\\log|X|,&amp;s=0,\\\\\n  |X|^{-s},&amp;0&lt;s&lt;2,\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\qquad\n  (k,\\gamma)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  (0,0),&amp;s=0,\\\\\n  (1,s-1),&amp;0&lt;s&lt;2.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n  Write \\(X=(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R^k\\), omit \\(y\\) when \\(k=0\\), and set\n  \\[\n  w_s=1\\quad(s=0),\\qquad w_s=|y|^\\gamma\\quad(s&gt;0).\n  \\]\n  The full-space normalization is\n  \\[\n  -\\operatorname{div}(w_s\\nabla g_s)=\\kappa_s\\delta_0,\n  \\qquad\n  \\kappa_0=2\\pi,\\qquad \\kappa_s=4\\pi\\quad(s&gt;0).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p\\) with\n  \\[\n  1&lt;p&lt;2\\quad(s=0),\\qquad\n  1&lt;p&lt;\\min\\!\\left(2,\\frac2s,\\frac3{s+1}\\right)\\quad(s&gt;0).\n  \\]\n  For a locally finite \\(C\\), admissible fields are gradients\n  \\[\n  E=\\nabla h\\in L^p_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^{2+k};\\mathbb R^{2+k}),\n  \\qquad w_sE\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}},\n  \\]\n  satisfying\n  \\[\n  -\\operatorname{div}(w_sE)\n  =\\kappa_s\\left(\\sum_{a\\in C}\\delta_{(a,0)}-\\delta_{\\mathbb R^2}\\right).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  f_{s,\\eta}=(g_s-g_s(\\eta))_+,\\qquad\n  E_\\eta=E-\\sum_{a\\in C}\\nabla f_{s,\\eta}(X-(a,0)),\n  \\qquad K_R=[-R/2,R/2]^2,\n  \\]\n  and define, in this order,\n  \\[\n  W_{s,\\eta}(E)\n  =\\limsup_{R\\to\\infty}\n  \\left[\\frac1{R^2}\\int_{K_R\\times\\mathbb R^k}w_s|E_\\eta|^2-\\kappa_sg_s(\\eta)\\right],\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  W_s(E)=\\lim_{\\eta\\downarrow0}W_{s,\\eta}(E),\n  \\qquad W_s(C)=\\inf_E W_s(E).\n  \\]\n  The infimum is outside both limits and is \\(+\\infty\\) if no admissible field exists.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(L\\) is a period lattice with torus \\(T=\\mathbb R^2/L\\) of area \\(N\\in\\mathbb N\\), and \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_N\\) are distinct, let \\(H\\) be the canonical potential with mean-zero trace and finite truncated energy satisfying\n  \\[\n  -\\operatorname{div}(w_s\\nabla H)\n  =\\kappa_s\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^N\\delta_{(a_i,0)}-\\delta_T\\right).\n  \\]\n  Truncations include periodic images. Define\n  \\[\n  W_{s,L}(a_1,\\ldots,a_N)\n  =\\lim_{\\eta\\downarrow0}\n  \\left[\\frac1N\\int_{T\\times\\mathbb R^k}w_s|\\nabla H_\\eta|^2-\\kappa_sg_s(\\eta)\\right].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(N\\in\\mathbb N\\), put \\(R=\\sqrt N\\), and define the finite ordered-pair jellium energy\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  J_{s,R}(a_1,\\ldots,a_N)\n  ={}&amp;\\sum_{i\\ne j}g_s(a_i-a_j)\n  -2\\sum_{i=1}^N\\int_{K_R}g_s(a_i-x)\\,dx\\\\\n  &amp;+\\int_{K_R}\\int_{K_R}g_s(x-x')\\,dx\\,dx'.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  Collisions have energy \\(+\\infty\\). The density-one thermodynamic minimum is\n  \\[\n  e_{\\mathrm{Jel}}^{(s)}\n  =\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac1N\n  \\min_{a_1,\\ldots,a_N\\in K_{\\sqrt N}}J_{s,\\sqrt N}(a_1,\\ldots,a_N).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every positive integer \\(n\\), put \\(N=n^2\\) and enumerate \\(A/(nA)\\) by \\(a_1^\\triangle,\\ldots,a_N^\\triangle\\). Every configuration of \\(N\\) distinct points in \\(\\mathbb R^2/(nA)\\) satisfies\n\\[\nW_{s,nA}(a_1,\\ldots,a_N)\n\\ge W_{s,nA}(a_1^\\triangle,\\ldots,a_N^\\triangle).\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(A\\) minimizes \\(W_s\\) among density-one planar configurations, the thermodynamic limit above exists, and\n\\[\ne_{\\mathrm{Jel}}^{(s)}=\\kappa_s^{-1}W_s(A).\n\\]\nNo uniqueness, exclusion of defects, assertion at \\(s=2\\), or quantum crystallization statement is included.</p>", "proof": "<p>First compare periodic configurations. For any simple density-one periodic \\(C\\), periodic counting gives\n\\[\nH_C(t)=\\frac1{4\\pi t}E_{g_t}(C),\n\\qquad g_t(u)=e^{-u/(4t)}.\n\\]\nThe subtraction removes only the coincident particle; all other images, including nonzero images of the same point, remain. Every fixed image displacement has its periodic frequency in large disks, and Gaussian tails are summable uniformly over the finitely many point types.</p><p>Theorem 1, or equivalently its Gaussian comparison in Proposition 7, gives\n\\[\nH_C(t)\\ge H_A(t)\\qquad(t&gt;0).\n\\]\nProposition 13 therefore yields\n\\[\nV_s(C)\\ge V_s(A)\n\\]\nfor every period lattice. Repeating \\(A\\) on \\(nA\\) preserves \\(H_A\\), hence its canonical periodic value, proving the stated triangular-torus minimum.</p><p>For the infinite-system field infimum, put\n\\[\nm_s=\\inf_EW_s(E)\n\\]\nover all compatible simple gradients in the fixed-\\(p\\), unit-background class, without adding a centered-density condition. Proposition 12 supplies canonical square-periodic fields with values tending to \\(m_s\\). Every such value is at least \\(V_s(A)\\) by the periodic heat comparison, so \\(m_s\\ge V_s(A)\\). The canonical field of \\(A\\) is admissible, so the reverse inequality holds. Consequently\n\\[\nm_s\\le W_s(A)\\le V_s(A)=m_s.\n\\]\nEvery configuration has \\(W_s(C)\\ge m_s\\) by inclusion in this field class. This does not require conversion between disk and square density or approximation with triangular periods.</p><p>It remains to identify the scalar jellium value. Let \\(G_L\\) be the mean-zero unit-source Green function of \\((-\\Delta)^{1-s/2}\\), and define the physical periodic kernel and its regular part by\n\\[\nv_L=q_sG_L,\\qquad r_L=\\lim_{x\\to0}(v_L(x)-g_s(x)).\n\\]\nThe unordered scalar periodic energy is\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{per},L}\n=\\sum_{i&lt;j}v_L(a_i-a_j)+\\frac N2r_L.\n\\]\nThe finite normalization identity is\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{per},L}}N\n=\\frac{W_{s,L}}{\\kappa_s}.\n\\]</p><p>The scalar thermodynamic inputs cited in the paper are, in this notation,\n\\[\n\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac1{2N}\n\\min_{a\\in(\\mathbb R^2)^N}J_{s,\\sqrt N}(a)\n=\n\\liminf_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac1N\n\\min_{a\\in(\\mathbb R^2/(\\sqrt N\\mathbb Z^2))^N}\n\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{per},\\sqrt N\\mathbb Z^2}(a)\n=:e_{\\mathrm{per}}^{(s)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;s&lt;2\\), the input is Lewin–Lieb–Seiringer, as specified in paper reference [20]; for \\(s=0\\), it is Lauritsen, paper reference [18], which also supplies existence of the full periodic limit. These inputs use \\(N\\) particles against unit background of area \\(N\\), unrestricted positions on the left, unordered pairs, zero-mean periodic kernels, and self term \\(Nr_L/2\\).</p><p>Every canonical periodic value is at least \\(m_s\\), and Proposition 12 supplies a cubic sequence tending to it. The normalization identity therefore identifies\n\\[\ne_{\\mathrm{per}}^{(s)}=\\frac{m_s}{2\\kappa_s}.\n\\]</p><p>Finally, finite minimization is unchanged by confining particles to the background square. Fix the other \\(N-1\\) particles. For \\(0&lt;s&lt;2\\), their one-particle potential extends to \\(\\mathbb R^3\\) as\n\\[\nU(X)=\\sum_{j\\ne i}|X-(a_j,0)|^{-s}\n-\\int_{K_R}|X-(y,0)|^{-s}\\,dy.\n\\]\nThe background potential is continuous because \\(s&lt;2\\), poles tend to \\(+\\infty\\), and\n\\[\nU(X)=-|X|^{-s}+O(|X|^{-s-1})\n\\]\nat infinity. Hence a negative global minimum is attained away from poles.</p><p>On the connected open set\n\\[\nD=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\n\\left((K_R\\times\\{0\\})\\cup\\{(a_j,0):j\\ne i\\}\\right),\n\\]\nthe potential is a continuous weak solution in\n\\(H^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(D,|z|^{s-1}dX)\\) of\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}(|z|^{s-1}\\nabla U)=0.\n\\]\nAcross source-free parts of \\(z=0\\), \\(\\partial_zU=O(|z|)\\), so weighted normal flux is \\(O(|z|^s)\\to0\\). The weighted strong minimum principle for \\(-1&lt;s-1&lt;1\\), obtained from the \\(A_2\\)-weight Harnack inequality cited in paper reference [13], forbids an interior minimum unless \\(U\\) is constant. Constancy is incompatible with a negative minimum and \\(U\\to0\\). Thus a minimum lies on \\(K_R\\times\\{0\\}\\).</p><p>For \\(s=0\\), ordinary harmonicity outside the square and poles, together with\n\\[\nU(x)=\\log|x|+o(1)\n\\]\nat infinity, gives the same conclusion by the ordinary strong minimum principle. Move each exterior particle to such a minimum. Energy does not increase and no collision is created. On \\(K_R^N\\), the extended energy is lower semicontinuous, finite at a distinct tuple, and diverges at collisions, so the infimum is attained. Confined and unrestricted minima agree.</p><p>The scalar thermodynamic equality now proves existence of the stated confined limit and gives\n\\[\ne_{\\mathrm{Jel}}^{(s)}\n=2e_{\\mathrm{per}}^{(s)}\n=\\frac{m_s}{\\kappa_s}\n=\\frac{W_s(A)}{\\kappa_s}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every nonzero shell of \\(A\\), and of \\(A^*\\), belongs to \\(\\mathcal N\\) in the coordinate \\(s=b|x|^2\\), and \\(A^*\\) is a rotation of \\(A\\).", "statement": "<p>**Cardinal interpolation, Fourier transformation, and distant-output bounds — paper Section 2.**</p><p>Definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Use\n  \\[\n  \\widehat f(\\xi)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}f(x)e^{-2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi}\\,dx.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  b=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2},\\quad h=\\frac25,\\quad B=\\frac43=\\frac1{b^2},\n  \\quad s=b|x|^2,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  L=\\{0,1,3,4,7,9\\},\\quad\n  \\mathcal Z=12\\mathbb Z+L,\\quad\n  \\mathcal N=\\mathcal Z\\cap(0,\\infty),\\quad\n  \\mathcal N_0=\\{0\\}\\cup\\mathcal N.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  P(s)=\\prod_{a\\in L}\\left(2\\sin\\frac{\\pi(s-a)}{12}\\right)^2\n  =\\sum_{\\ell=-6}^6P_\\ell e^{i\\pi t_\\ell s},\n  \\qquad t_\\ell=\\frac{\\ell}{6},\n  \\]\n  where\n  \\[\n  (P_0,\\ldots,P_6)\n  =\\left(5,-1+2ib,1+2ib,-2,-\\frac12+ib,-1-2ib,1\\right),\n  \\quad P_{-\\ell}=\\overline{P_\\ell}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(n\\in\\mathcal N_0\\), define \\(Q_n,D_n\\) by\n  \\[\n  P(n+u)=Q_nu^2(1+D_nu+O(u^2)).\n  \\]\n  Their values depend on \\(a=n\\bmod12\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n  a&amp;Q_n&amp;D_n\\\\ \\hline\n  0&amp;\\pi^2/3&amp;-7\\pi\\sqrt3/18\\\\\n  1&amp;(2\\pi^2/3)(2-\\sqrt3)&amp;\\pi(3+\\sqrt3)/18\\\\\n  3&amp;(2\\pi^2/3)(2-\\sqrt3)&amp;-\\pi(3+\\sqrt3)/18\\\\\n  4&amp;\\pi^2/3&amp;7\\pi\\sqrt3/18\\\\\n  7&amp;(2\\pi^2/3)(2+\\sqrt3)&amp;-\\pi(3-\\sqrt3)/18\\\\\n  9&amp;(2\\pi^2/3)(2+\\sqrt3)&amp;\\pi(3-\\sqrt3)/18.\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A real coefficient list \\(a=(C,(c_n,d_n)_{n\\in\\mathcal N_0})\\) has norm\n  \\[\n  \\|a\\|_1=|C|+\\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal N_0}(|c_n|+|d_n|)&lt;\\infty.\n  \\]\n  Away from the nodes, put\n  \\[\n  p_a(s)=P(s)\\left(C+\\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal N_0}\n  \\left(\\frac{c_n}{(s-n)^2}+\\frac{d_n}{s-n}\\right)\\right).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  z(t)=-\\frac{B}{t+ih}-ih,\\qquad\n  \\lambda(t)=\\frac{i}{b(t+ih)}.\n  \\]\n  The measure \\(\\mu_a\\) and profile \\(Kp_a\\) are specified in Lemma 1.2.</p><p class=\"li\">For differentiable \\(u\\), define its positive-node jet coordinates by\n  \\[\n  (\\mathcal Ju)_{c,n}=\\frac{u(n)}{Q_n},\\qquad\n  (\\mathcal Ju)_{d,n}=\\frac{u'(n)-D_nu(n)}{Q_n},\n  \\qquad n\\in\\mathcal N,\n  \\]\n  and set \\(Sa=-\\mathcal J Kp_a\\). The coordinates \\((c_0,d_0,C)\\) are called extras.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every nonzero shell of \\(A\\), and of \\(A^*\\), belongs to \\(\\mathcal N\\) in the coordinate \\(s=b|x|^2\\), and \\(A^*\\) is a rotation of \\(A\\). The cardinal profiles extend to entire functions, have the specified node jets and compact spectral representations, and yield genuine real radial Schwartz Fourier pairs. The operator \\(S\\), including extra inputs if desired, is bounded into positive-node \\(\\ell^1\\), has norm below \\(4000\\), and has the uniform distant-output and derivative bounds of Lemma 1.4.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the triangular basis\n\\[\nu=\\sqrt{2/\\sqrt3}(1,0),\\qquad\nv=\\sqrt{2/\\sqrt3}(1/2,\\sqrt3/2).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nb|ju+\\ell v|^2=j^2+j\\ell+\\ell^2.\n\\]\nModulo \\(3\\), this is \\((j-\\ell)^2\\), hence \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). Modulo \\(2\\), it is even only when \\(j,\\ell\\) are both even, in which case it is divisible by \\(4\\). These restrictions give the six residues in \\(L\\). The containment is strict: if the form had value \\(24\\), division by \\(4\\) and the same parity implication at value \\(6\\) would force \\(16\\mid24\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Let \\(J\\) be positive right-angle rotation. Since \\(\\det(u,v)=1\\), the vectors \\(-Jv,Ju\\) are dual to \\(u,v\\) and generate \\(JA\\). Thus \\(A^*=JA\\).</p><p>Writing \\(w=e^{i\\pi s/6}\\) expresses the sine product as\n\\[\nw^{-6}\\prod_{a\\in L}(w-e^{i\\pi a/6})^2.\n\\]\nThe scalar factor is one because \\(\\sum_{a\\in L}a=24\\); multiplication gives the stated Fourier coefficients. Removing the vanishing factor yields\n\\[\nQ_a=\\left(\\frac\\pi6\\right)^2\n\\prod_{d\\in L\\setminus\\{a\\}}\n\\left(2\\sin\\frac{\\pi(a-d)}{12}\\right)^2,\n\\qquad\nD_a=\\frac\\pi6\\sum_{d\\in L\\setminus\\{a\\}}\n\\cot\\frac{\\pi(a-d)}{12}.\n\\]\nAngle-addition formulas give the table, and Lemma 1.1 gives its strict bounds. Lemmas 1.2–1.4 prove the spectral, Fourier, and operator assertions.</p><p>In particular, \\(\\mathcal Jp_a\\) is the positive-node part \\(a^+\\), while extras contribute no direct positive-node jets. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal JH_1=a_1^+-Sa_2,\\qquad\n\\mathcal JH_2=a_2^+-Sa_1.\n\\]\nThese are the coupled interpolation equations used below.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Elementary constant enclosures — paper Lemma A.1.** Put \\(\\epsilon=10^{-80}\\), and let \\( p_*= 3.14159265358979323846264338327950288419716939937510582097494459230781640628620899, \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Elementary constant enclosures — paper Lemma A.1.**</p><p>Put \\(\\epsilon=10^{-80}\\), and let\n\\[\np_*=\n3.14159265358979323846264338327950288419716939937510582097494459230781640628620899,\n\\]\n\\[\nb_*=\n0.86602540378443864676372317075293618347140262690519031402790348972596650845440001.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\np_*&lt;\\pi&lt;p_*+\\epsilon,\\qquad b_*&lt;\\sqrt3/2&lt;b_*+\\epsilon.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\frac{157}{50}&lt;\\pi&lt;\\frac{22}{7},\\qquad\n\\sqrt3&lt;\\frac{1733}{1000},\\qquad\n\\pi\\left(\\frac2{\\sqrt3}-\\frac25\\right)&gt;2.36,\n\\]\nand the node constants in Proposition 1 satisfy\n\\[\nQ_n&gt;\\frac74=1.75,\\qquad |D_n|&lt;\\frac{53}{25}=2.12.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\nA(x)=\\sum_{j=0}^{65}\\frac{(-1)^jx^{2j+1}}{2j+1}.\n\\]\nIntegrating the finite geometric expansion of \\(1/(1+x^2)\\) gives, for \\(0&lt;x&lt;1\\),\n\\[\nA(x)&lt;\\arctan x&lt;A(x)+\\frac{x^{133}}{133}.\n\\]\nThe tangent addition formula gives\n\\[\n\\tan(4\\arctan(1/5))=\\frac{120}{119},\n\\qquad\n\\frac\\pi4=4\\arctan(1/5)-\\arctan(1/239).\n\\]\nThe latter angle lies in \\((0,\\pi/2)\\) and has tangent one. Clearing positive denominators verifies\n\\[\np_*&lt;16A(1/5)-4\\left(A(1/239)+\\frac{239^{-133}}{133}\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\n16\\left(A(1/5)+\\frac{5^{-133}}{133}\\right)-4A(1/239)&lt;p_*+\\epsilon.\n\\]\nThe enclosure of \\(b\\) is the pair of integer-square comparisons\n\\[\nb_*^2&lt;\\frac34&lt;(b_*+\\epsilon)^2.\n\\]</p><p>Polynomial division gives\n\\[\n0&lt;\\int_0^1\\frac{x^4(1-x)^4}{1+x^2}\\,dx=\\frac{22}{7}-\\pi.\n\\]\nThe shorter Machin estimate is\n\\[\n\\pi&gt;16\\left(\\frac15-\\frac1{3\\cdot5^3}\\right)-\\frac4{239}\n=\\frac{281476}{89625}&gt;\\frac{157}{50}.\n\\]\nSquaring gives \\(\\sqrt3&lt;1733/1000\\), and hence\n\\[\n\\pi\\left(\\frac2{\\sqrt3}-\\frac25\\right)\n&gt;\\frac{157}{50}\\left(\\frac{2000}{1733}-\\frac25\\right)\n=\\frac{1025838}{433250}&gt;\\frac{59}{25}.\n\\]\nThe smallest \\(Q_n\\) exceeds\n\\[\n\\frac23\\left(\\frac{157}{50}\\right)^2\\frac{267}{1000}&gt;\\frac74,\n\\]\nwhile the largest \\(|D_n|\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac{7\\pi\\sqrt3}{18}\n&lt;\\frac{22}{18}\\frac{1733}{1000}&lt;\\frac{53}{25}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Cardinal measure — paper Lemma 2.2.** For every coefficient list \\(a\\) of Proposition 1, \\(p_a\\) extends to an entire function, real on the real axis, with …", "statement": "<p>**Cardinal measure — paper Lemma 2.2.**</p><p>For every coefficient list \\(a\\) of Proposition 1, \\(p_a\\) extends to an entire function, real on the real axis, with\n\\[\np_a(n)=Q_nc_n,\\qquad\np_a'(n)=Q_n(d_n+D_nc_n)\\qquad(n\\in\\mathcal N_0).\n\\]\nThere is a finite complex measure \\(\\mu_a\\) on \\([-1,1]\\) such that\n\\[\np_a(s)=\\int_{-1}^1e^{i\\pi ts}\\,d\\mu_a(t)\\qquad(s\\in\\mathbb C),\n\\quad\n\\mu_a(-E)=\\overline{\\mu_a(E)},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|\\mu_a\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n\\le25|C|+36\\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal N_0}(|c_n|+|d_n|).\n\\]\nFor real \\(s\\), a folded measure \\(\\nu_a\\) on \\([0,1]\\) satisfies\n\\[\np_a(s)=\\operatorname{Re}\\int_0^1e^{i\\pi ts}\\,d\\nu_a(t).\n\\]\nIts atoms are \\(Ce_jP_j\\) at \\(t_j=j/6\\), where \\(e_0=1\\), \\(e_j=2\\) for \\(j&gt;0\\), and its density on \\(t_{j-1}&lt;t&lt;t_j\\) is\n\\[\nd_j(t)=2\\pi\\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal N_0}e^{-i\\pi tn}\n\\sum_{\\ell=j}^6[-\\pi c_n(t_\\ell-t)+id_n]P_\\ell e^{i\\pi t_\\ell n}.\n\\]\nThe same variation bound holds for \\(\\nu_a\\). Define\n\\[\nKp_a(s)=\\int_{-1}^1\\lambda(t)e^{i\\pi z(t)s}\\,d\\mu_a(t).\n\\]\nThen \\(Kp_a\\) is entire and real on the real axis, where\n\\[\nKp_a(s)=\\operatorname{Re}\\int_0^1\\lambda(t)e^{i\\pi z(t)s}\\,d\\nu_a(t).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For one node \\(n\\), write \\(u=s-n\\). Direct integration gives the entire identities\n\\[\n\\frac{e^{i\\pi t_\\ell u}-1}{u}\n=i\\pi\\int_0^{t_\\ell}e^{i\\pi tu}\\,dt,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{e^{i\\pi t_\\ell u}-1-i\\pi t_\\ell u}{u^2}\n=-\\pi^2\\int_0^{t_\\ell}(t_\\ell-t)e^{i\\pi tu}\\,dt.\n\\]\nMultiply by \\(P_\\ell e^{i\\pi t_\\ell n}\\) and sum over \\(\\ell\\). The constant and linear subtractions cancel because \\(P(n)=P'(n)=0\\). This supplies measures for both node fractions. Their negative-frequency portions are conjugate reflections of the positive-frequency portions. Folding doubles the latter and gives the displayed density. Node measures are absolutely continuous; only \\(CP\\) contributes atoms.</p><p>The coefficient moduli are\n\\[\n(|P_1|,\\ldots,|P_6|)=(2,2,2,1,2,1).\n\\]\nThus the constant column has variation \\(25\\), and each unit \\(c_n\\) or \\(d_n\\) column has variation at most, respectively,\n\\[\n\\pi^2\\sum_{\\ell=1}^6t_\\ell^2|P_\\ell|\n=\\frac{65\\pi^2}{18}&lt;36,\n\\qquad\n2\\pi\\sum_{\\ell=1}^6t_\\ell|P_\\ell|\n=\\frac{32\\pi}{3}&lt;36.\n\\]\nThe phases involving \\(n\\) have modulus one on real frequencies, so these bounds are independent of \\(n\\).</p><p>Absolute summability makes the measures converge in total variation. On compact subsets of the complex \\(s\\)-plane, the exponential and every derivative are uniformly bounded for \\(|t|\\le1\\). The integrals and their derivatives therefore converge locally uniformly. Away from nodes, the cardinal series also converges uniformly on compact sets. The integral gives its entire extension.</p><p>For finite lists, the expansion of \\(P\\) at \\(n\\) gives the stated jets; every other node term vanishes to second order. Locally uniform convergence passes these identities to summable lists. Conjugate symmetry proves reality and folding.</p><p>The image of \\([-1,1]\\) under \\(z\\) is compact and \\(\\lambda\\) is bounded, so the same argument makes \\(Kp_a\\) entire. Finally,\n\\[\n\\lambda(-t)=\\overline{\\lambda(t)},\\qquad z(-t)=-\\overline{z(t)}\n\\]\ngive its reality and folded formula. The real-part formulas are evaluation rules for real \\(s\\), not definitions at complex arguments.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Fourier pairs — paper Lemma 2.4, with the pointwise consistency observation of paper Remark 2.3.** For every coefficient list \\(a\\), the radial functions, with \\(s=b|x|^2\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Fourier pairs — paper Lemma 2.4, with the pointwise consistency observation of paper Remark 2.3.**</p><p>For every coefficient list \\(a\\), the radial functions, with \\(s=b|x|^2\\),\n\\[\ne^{-\\pi hs}p_a(s),\\qquad e^{-\\pi hs}Kp_a(s)\n\\]\nare real Schwartz functions on \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) and Fourier transforms of one another. Consequently, for two lists \\(a_1,a_2\\),\n\\[\nH_1=p_{a_1}+Kp_{a_2},\\qquad\nH_2=p_{a_2}+Kp_{a_1}\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\nf(x)=e^{-\\pi hb|x|^2}H_1(b|x|^2),\n\\qquad\n\\widehat f(\\xi)=e^{-\\pi hb|\\xi|^2}H_2(b|\\xi|^2).\n\\]\nPointwise,\n\\[\nz(z(t))=t,\\qquad \\lambda(t)\\lambda(z(t))=1\n\\]\nwhenever the expressions are defined. These identities do not define composition of the folded real-contour integral.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(\\operatorname{Re}\\gamma&gt;0\\), the dimension-two Gaussian transform is\n\\[\n\\widehat{e^{-\\pi\\gamma|x|^2}}(\\xi)=\\gamma^{-1}e^{-\\pi|\\xi|^2/\\gamma}.\n\\]\nFor positive real \\(\\gamma\\), squaring the Gaussian integral and using polar coordinates gives the one-dimensional value \\(\\gamma^{-1/2}\\) at zero. Integration by parts gives\n\\[\nF'(\\xi)=-\\frac{2\\pi\\xi}{\\gamma}F(\\xi).\n\\]\nSolving and taking the product in two coordinates proves the formula. Holomorphy on the right half-plane and locally justified differentiation under the integral extend it to complex \\(\\gamma\\).</p><p>Multiplying the spectral integrand of \\(p_a\\) by \\(e^{-\\pi hs}\\) gives parameter\n\\[\n\\gamma=b(h-it)=-ib(t+ih).\n\\]\nSince \\(B=1/b^2\\), its transform is\n\\[\n\\frac1{b(h-it)}\n\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\pi|\\xi|^2}{b(h-it)}\\right)\n=e^{-\\pi hs}\\lambda(t)e^{i\\pi z(t)s},\n\\qquad s=b|\\xi|^2.\n\\]\nIntegration against \\(\\mu_a\\) proves the Fourier formula. All exchanges with Fourier integration, derivatives, and polynomial weights are uniformly justified by a fixed polynomial times a decaying Gaussian. On the transformed side, the real part of the Gaussian parameter is\n\\[\n\\frac{h}{b(h^2+t^2)},\n\\]\nwhich has a positive lower bound on \\([-1,1]\\). Both functions are Schwartz. Fourier transformation twice is reflection, which fixes radial functions; hence the reverse transform and the two-list assertion follow.</p><p>Because \\(z(t)+ih=-B/(t+ih)\\), direct substitution gives the pointwise consistency identities. The map \\(z\\) does not preserve the real contour, and these substitutions do not supply a cardinal measure for a transformed profile on that contour.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Kernel, tail, and derivative bounds — paper Lemma 2.5 and equations (2.27)–(2.28).** For \\(0\\le t\\le1\\), \\( |\\lambda(t)|<3,\\qquad |z(t)|\\le\\frac{44}{15}<3,\\qquad \\pi\\operatorname{Im}z(t)>.187. \\) The last quantity …", "statement": "<p>**Kernel, tail, and derivative bounds — paper Lemma 2.5 and equations (2.27)–(2.28).**</p><p>For \\(0\\le t\\le1\\),\n\\[\n|\\lambda(t)|&lt;3,\\qquad |z(t)|\\le\\frac{44}{15}&lt;3,\\qquad\n\\pi\\operatorname{Im}z(t)&gt;.187.\n\\]\nThe last quantity exceeds \\(.70\\) on \\(0\\le t\\le5/6\\). On \\(5/6\\le t\\le1\\),\n\\[\n|\\lambda(t)|&lt;1.25,\\qquad |z(t)|&lt;1.32,\\qquad\n\\pi\\operatorname{Im}z(t)&gt;.187+2.48(1-t).\n\\]</p><p>If\n\\[\nw\\ge\\sum_n(|c_n|+|d_n|),\\qquad c\\ge|C|,\n\\]\nthen, for every positive integer \\(r\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\in\\mathcal N\\\\n\\ge r}}\n\\bigl(|(Sa)_{c,n}|+|(Sa)_{d,n}|\\bigr)\n\\le U_r(w,c),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nU_r(w,c)\n=800(w+c)\\frac{e^{-.70r}}{1-e^{-.70}}\n+5.3\\left(\\frac{2.54w}{r}+2c\\right)\n\\frac{e^{-.187r}}{1-e^{-.187}}.\n\\]\nThe same expression bounds summed outputs for several lists when \\(w,c\\) bound their summed inputs. Moreover \\(\\|S\\|&lt;4000\\).</p><p>If\n\\[\nV=25|C|+36\\sum_n(|c_n|+|d_n|),\n\\]\nthen for every \\(j\\ge0\\), \\(s\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n|p_a^{(j)}(s)|\\le\\pi^jV,\\qquad\n|(Kp_a)^{(j)}(s)|\\le3(3\\pi)^je^{-.187s}V.\n\\]\nFor \\(s&gt;0\\), the sharper bound is\n\\[\n|(Kp_a)^{(j)}(s)|\n\\le3(3\\pi)^j(36w+25c)e^{-.70s}\n+1.25(1.32\\pi)^j\\left(\\frac{2.54w}{s}+2c\\right)e^{-.187s}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(q(t)=\\pi\\operatorname{Im}z(t)\\). Direct calculation gives\n\\[\n|\\lambda(t)|=\\frac1{b\\sqrt{t^2+h^2}},\n\\quad\n|z(t)|^2=h^2+\\frac{B^2-2Bh^2}{t^2+h^2},\n\\quad\nq(t)=\\pi h\\left(\\frac B{t^2+h^2}-1\\right).\n\\]\nThese decrease for \\(t\\ge0\\), since \\(B&gt;2h^2\\). At zero,\n\\[\n|\\lambda(0)|=\\frac5{\\sqrt3}&lt;3,\\qquad |z(0)|=\\frac{44}{15}.\n\\]\nThe endpoint evaluations are\n\\[\nq(1)=\\frac{26\\pi}{435}&gt;.187,\\qquad\nq(5/6)=\\frac{862\\pi}{3845}&gt;.70,\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\lambda(5/6)|=\\frac{60}{\\sqrt{2307}}&lt;\\frac54,\n\\qquad\n|z(5/6)|^2=\\frac{33476}{19225}&lt;\\left(\\frac{33}{25}\\right)^2.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\n-q'(t)=\\frac{2\\pi hBt}{(t^2+h^2)^2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(t/(t^2+h^2)^2\\) decreases for \\(t&gt;h/\\sqrt3\\),\n\\[\n-q'(t)\\ge\\frac{2000\\pi}{2523}&gt;2.48\n\\quad(5/6\\le t\\le1).\n\\]\nIntegrating proves the sharpened exponent.</p><p>One unit of spectral variation contributes at most\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\lambda(t)|}{Q_n}\n(1+|D_n|+\\pi|z(t)|)e^{-q(t)n}\n\\]\nto the two absolute jet coordinates at \\(n\\). Its prefactor is below \\(22\\) globally and below \\(5.3\\) on the last interval. The variation is at most \\(36\\|a\\|_1\\). Summation over all positive integers gives\n\\[\n\\|S\\|\\le\\frac{22\\cdot36}{e^{.187}-1}&lt;4000,\n\\]\nusing \\(e^{.187}-1&gt;.187+(.187)^2/2&gt;.198\\).</p><p>Split the folded measure into \\([0,5/6]\\) and \\((5/6,1]\\), assigning the atom at \\(5/6\\) to the first. The first part contributes at most\n\\[\n22(36w+25c)e^{-.70n}\\le800(w+c)e^{-.70n}.\n\\]\nOn the last open interval, only \\(\\ell=6\\) remains in the density; its modulus is at most \\(2\\pi w\\). The only atom there is \\(2C\\) at \\(1\\). This part contributes at most\n\\[\n5.3e^{-.187n}\n\\left(2\\pi w\\int_{5/6}^1e^{-2.48n(1-t)}\\,dt+2c\\right)\n\\le5.3e^{-.187n}\\left(\\frac{2.54w}{n}+2c\\right).\n\\]\nSumming with \\(1/n\\le1/r\\) gives \\(U_r\\). Differentiating the spectral integrals supplies the derivative estimates, with factors \\(i\\pi t\\) and \\(i\\pi z(t)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The exact arrays satisfy \\( \\|D^{64}\\|<.004,\\qquad \\|V_+\\|+\\|V_-\\|<36, \\) \\( \\sum_{i=1}^2\\|(X_i)_j\\|_1< \\begin{cases} 2.3,&1\\le j\\le5,\\\\ 5.6,&6\\le j\\le10. \\end{cases} \\) For every specified half-gap, every \\(i=1,2\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Positive quadrature and the finite certificate — paper Proposition 3.3.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Retain the setting of Proposition 1. Put\n  \\[\n  M=168,\\qquad \\mathcal F=\\mathcal N\\cap[1,M],\\qquad N_{\\mathrm q}=384.\n  \\]\n  There are \\(84\\) finite nodes and \\(168\\) positive-node coordinates, ordered first by all \\(c_n\\), then all \\(d_n\\), with increasing \\(n\\). Append \\(e=(c_0,d_0,C)\\), giving \\(171\\) input coordinates.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0\\le v&lt;N_{\\mathrm q}\\), \\(1\\le j\\le6\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\alpha_v=\\frac{v+1/2}{N_{\\mathrm q}},\\qquad\n  \\tau_{jv}=\\frac{2j-1+\\cos(\\pi\\alpha_v)}{12},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  o_v=\\frac1{N_{\\mathrm q}}\n  \\left(1-2\\sum_{a=1}^{N_{\\mathrm q}/2-1}\n  \\frac{\\cos(2a\\pi\\alpha_v)}{4a^2-1}\\right).\n  \\]\n  Replace the density integral on the \\(j\\)-th interval by masses \\(o_vd_j(\\tau_{jv})/6\\), keeping all atoms exact. A tilde denotes this replacement.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W\\) be the \\(2311\\times171\\) complex mass matrix, with \\(2304\\) quadrature rows followed by seven atoms. For \\(t=\\tau_{jv}\\), \\(n\\in\\mathcal F\\cup\\{0\\}\\),\n  \\[\n  W_{(j,v),c_n}\n  =-\\frac{2\\pi^2o_v}{6}e^{-i\\pi tn}\n  \\sum_{\\ell=j}^6(t_\\ell-t)P_\\ell e^{i\\pi t_\\ell n},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  W_{(j,v),d_n}\n  =\\frac{2\\pi io_v}{6}e^{-i\\pi tn}\n  \\sum_{\\ell=j}^6P_\\ell e^{i\\pi t_\\ell n},\n  \\qquad W_{(j,v),C}=0.\n  \\]\n  At atom \\(j\\), only \\(W_{\\mathrm{atom}(j),C}=e_jP_j\\) is nonzero.</p><p class=\"li\">For the tuple \\(t\\) of all mass points, define\n  \\[\n  F_a(m,y;t)=e^{i\\pi tm}\\frac{(i\\pi yt)^a}{a!},\n  \\qquad\n  G_a(m,y;t)=\\lambda(t)e^{i\\pi z(t)m}\n  \\frac{(i\\pi yz(t))^a}{a!}.\n  \\]\n  Products are ordinary products without complex conjugation.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  (\\widetilde S_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal F},\\widetilde S_{\\mathcal Fe})\n  =-\\mathcal J_{\\mathcal F}\\widetilde K,\\qquad\n  D=\\widetilde S_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal F}.\n  \\]\n  Thus if \\(a_n=\\operatorname{Re}(G_0(n,1)W)\\),\n  \\(b_n=\\operatorname{Re}(G_1(n,1)W)\\), the projected rows are\n  \\[\n  -a_n/Q_n,\\qquad(D_na_n-b_n)/Q_n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(n_1,\\ldots,n_5=(1,3,4,7,9)\\),\n  \\[\n  v_j=e^{-k(n_j-1)},\\qquad\n  q(k)=(v_1,\\ldots,v_5,v_1/k,\\ldots,v_5/k)^t.\n  \\]\n  The \\(3\\times10\\) matrices \\(E_1,E_2\\) are zero except in columns \\(1,6\\), and are defined so that\n  \\[\n  \\binom{E_1q(k)}{E_2q(k)}\n  =\n  \\begin{pmatrix}.567\\\\.250\\\\-.0074\\\\0\\\\-.209\\\\.0096\\end{pmatrix}\n  +\\frac1k\n  \\begin{pmatrix}-.976\\\\.408\\\\.0115\\\\1\\\\.938\\\\-.017\\end{pmatrix}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the \\(168\\times10\\) matrix \\(Y\\) by\n  \\[\n  Y_{d_{n_j},j}=-Q_{n_j}^{-1},\\quad\n  Y_{c_{n_j},j+5}=Q_{n_j}^{-1},\\quad\n  Y_{d_{n_j},j+5}=-D_{n_j}/Q_{n_j}.\n  \\]\n  All other entries vanish. Set\n  \\[\n  g_1=Y+\\widetilde S_{\\mathcal Fe}E_2,\\qquad\n  g_2=\\widetilde S_{\\mathcal Fe}E_1,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  V_+=\\sum_{j=0}^{511}D^j,\\qquad V_-=\\sum_{j=0}^{511}(-D)^j,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  X_{1\\mathcal F}\n  =\\tfrac12\\bigl(V_+(g_1+g_2)+V_-(g_1-g_2)\\bigr),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  X_{2\\mathcal F}\n  =\\tfrac12\\bigl(V_+(g_1+g_2)-V_-(g_1-g_2)\\bigr),\n  \\qquad X_i=\\binom{X_{i\\mathcal F}}{E_i}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Parameter endpoints are \\(2.36,2.65,3.2,4,6,\\infty\\). For consecutive \\(K,J\\) and a real row \\(l=(a_1,\\ldots,a_5,b_1,\\ldots,b_5)\\), put\n  \\[\n  [l]_{K,J}\n  =\\min_{x\\in\\{1/K,1/J\\}}\n  \\left(a_1+xb_1+\\sum_{j=2}^5\n  \\min_{H\\in\\{K,J\\}}\\{(a_j+xb_j)e^{-H(n_j-1)}\\}\\right),\n  \\]\n  with \\(1/\\infty=0\\) and \\(e^{-\\infty(n_j-1)}=0\\) for \\(j&gt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each center \\(m\\in\\mathcal N_0\\cap[0,88]\\), use its half-gap to the successor and, if \\(m&gt;0\\), its half-gap to the predecessor. Write \\(s=m+yx\\), \\(0\\le x\\le1\\), where \\(y\\) is the signed half-gap length. Put \\(\\nu=0\\) at \\(m=0\\), \\(\\nu=2\\) otherwise, and \\(\\sigma_1=-1,\\sigma_2=1\\). Define\n  \\[\n  l_{i,r}\n  =\\sigma_i y^{-\\nu}\\operatorname{Re}\n  \\bigl(F_{r+\\nu}(m,y)WX_i+G_{r+\\nu}(m,y)WX_{3-i}\\bigr),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  L_{i,d}=\\sum_{r=0}^d\\omega_{dr}l_{i,r},\n  \\qquad \\omega_{dr}=\\frac{\\binom dr}{\\binom{40}r},\n  \\quad0\\le d\\le40.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For each half-gap and parameter box, define the target coefficients\n  \\[\n  (a_r^*)_{r=0}^{40}=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  ((e^K/K)(-Ky)^r/r!)_{r=0}^{40},&amp;m=0,\\\\\n  (K(-Ky)^r/(r+2)!)_{r=0}^{40},&amp;m=1,\\\\\n  ((J/2)e^{-13J/6},0,\\ldots,0),&amp;m=3,\\ J&lt;\\infty,\\\\\n  (0,\\ldots,0),&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(s_0=89.5\\), \\(\\mathcal N_{\\le88}=\\mathcal N_0\\cap[0,88]\\). Let \\(u_{i,n},d^*_{i,n},C_i^*\\) be the \\(c_n,d_n,C\\) rows of \\(\\sigma_iX_i\\), and set \\(\\eta_{K,J}(l)=\\min(0,[l]_{K,J})\\). All terminating decimals are exact rationals. Matrix norms are maximum absolute column sums.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The exact arrays satisfy\n\\[\n\\|D^{64}\\|&lt;.004,\\qquad \\|V_+\\|+\\|V_-\\|&lt;36,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^2\\|(X_i)_j\\|_1&lt;\n\\begin{cases}\n2.3,&amp;1\\le j\\le5,\\\\\n5.6,&amp;6\\le j\\le10.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nFor every specified half-gap, every \\(i=1,2\\), every parameter box, and \\(0\\le d\\le40\\),\n\\[\n[L_{i,d}]_{K,J}\n+\\mathbf1_{i=1}\\sum_{r=0}^d\\omega_{dr}a_r^*&gt;.001.\n\\]\nFor every box and \\(i=1,2\\),\n\\[\n[C_i^*]_{K,J}\n+\\frac{\\eta_{K,J}(\\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal N_{\\le88}}d^*_{i,n})}{s_0}\n+\\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal N_{\\le88}}\n\\left(\n\\frac{\\eta_{K,J}(u_{i,n})}{(s_0-n)^2}\n+\\frac{n\\,\\eta_{K,J}(d^*_{i,n})}{s_0(s_0-n)}\n\\right)&gt;.0021.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 2.1–2.2 supply the specified positive rule and its analytic error. Differentiating its finite exponential sums gives the rows \\(F_aW,G_aW\\), with no conjugation. The target jets at the first five nodes are \\(Yq(k)\\). The extras leave direct positive-node jets unchanged but alter the transformed contribution. The finite coupled equations are\n\\[\nA_1-DA_2=Y+\\widetilde S_{\\mathcal Fe}E_2,\\qquad\nA_2-DA_1=\\widetilde S_{\\mathcal Fe}E_1.\n\\]\nAdding and subtracting gives the formulas using \\(V_+,V_-\\), because\n\\[\n(I-D)V_+=(I+D)V_-=I-D^{512}.\n\\]</p><p>The finite certificate is obtained by the paper's selected direct Arb enclosure of the exact quadrature arrays. No precomputed coefficient list or approximate auxiliary function is an input. All arrays are reconstructed from the displayed formulas.</p><p>For the powers, start \\(U=D,V_+=V_-=I\\). At step \\(r=0,\\ldots,8\\), replace\n\\[\nV_+\\leftarrow V_+(I+U),\\qquad\nV_-\\leftarrow V_-(I+\\varepsilon_rU),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon_0=-1\\), \\(\\varepsilon_r=1\\) otherwise, and square \\(U\\) before the next step. Before step \\(r\\), \\(U=D^{2^r}\\). The product identities give exactly the \\(512\\)-term sums. The strict stronger bounds are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|r|r|r}\nr&amp;\\|U\\|&amp;\\|V_+\\|&amp;\\|V_-\\|\\\\ \\hline\n0&amp;1.74&amp;2.74&amp;2.1\\\\\n1&amp;2.09&amp;6.94&amp;2.2\\\\\n2&amp;2.27&amp;15.32&amp;2.13\\\\\n3&amp;1.75&amp;25.3&amp;1.97\\\\\n4&amp;.75&amp;31.1&amp;1.88\\\\\n5&amp;.12&amp;32.09&amp;1.85\\\\\n6&amp;.0031&amp;32.12&amp;1.85\\\\\n7&amp;.000002&amp;32.12&amp;1.85\\\\\n8&amp;.000000000001&amp;32.12&amp;1.85.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe stronger column bounds are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n&amp;j=1,\\ldots,5&amp;j=6,\\ldots,10\\\\ \\hline\n\\max_j\\|(X_1)_j\\|_1&amp;1.29&amp;2.65\\\\\n\\max_j\\|(X_2)_j\\|_1&amp;1.01&amp;3.03\\\\\n\\max_j\\sum_i\\|(X_i)_j\\|_1&amp;2.20&amp;5.40.\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><p>There are \\(46\\) centers and \\(91\\) signed half-gaps. Lemma 2.3 converts the polynomial and parameter continuum into \\(91\\cdot2\\cdot5\\cdot41=37310\\) strict lower comparisons. The stronger lower cutoffs, each covering both functions, every indicated signed half-gap, and every Bernstein index, are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c|c}\nK&amp;m=0&amp;m=1&amp;m=3,4&amp;7\\le m\\le88&amp;\\text{tail}\\\\ \\hline\n2.36&amp;.33&amp;.019&amp;.0033&amp;.00116&amp;.0022\\\\\n2.65&amp;.34&amp;.017&amp;.0037&amp;.0018&amp;.0029\\\\\n3.2&amp;.35&amp;.033&amp;.0061&amp;.0026&amp;.0036\\\\\n4&amp;.36&amp;.036&amp;.0085&amp;.0031&amp;.0043\\\\\n6&amp;.38&amp;.011&amp;.0105&amp;.0037&amp;.0052.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThere are ten tail tests.</p><p>The local source \\(\\texttt{verification/numeric\\_balls.py}\\), as described in the paper, uses \\(256\\)-bit Arb balls to enclose the exact arrays and tests every index: an upper test requires the whole ball below its cutoff, and a lower test requires the whole ball above it. The paper reports that all comparisons pass. Continuum coverage is supplied by the containing-box and Bernstein arguments and the later analytic remainder estimates.</p><p>For the tail, use\n\\[\n\\frac1{s-n}=\\frac1s+\\frac{n}{s(s-n)}.\n\\]\nGroup the simple-pole rows into their common \\(1/s\\) numerator before taking negative parts. For \\(s\\ge s_0\\), all reciprocal factors are positive and decreasing, so multiplying a nonpositive lower numerator bound is smallest at \\(s_0\\). This gives the tested expression.</p><p>The stronger cutoffs imply the proposition:\n\\[\n.0031&lt;.004,\\quad32.12+1.85&lt;36,\\quad\n2.20&lt;2.3,\\quad5.40&lt;5.6,\\quad\n.00116&gt;.001,\\quad .0022&gt;.0021.\n\\]\nLemma 2.4 supplies the separate conditional rational route. That full matrix construction is not executed by the supplied programs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Exactness and positivity of the quadrature — paper Section 3.1.** The quadrature of Proposition 2 is exact through degree \\(383\\) on each frequency interval, with interval mass \\(1/6\\).", "statement": "<p>**Exactness and positivity of the quadrature — paper Section 3.1.**</p><p>The quadrature of Proposition 2 is exact through degree \\(383\\) on each frequency interval, with interval mass \\(1/6\\). Its weights satisfy\n\\[\no_v\\ge\\frac1{N_{\\mathrm q}(N_{\\mathrm q}-1)}&gt;0,\n\\qquad \\sum_{v=0}^{N_{\\mathrm q}-1}o_v=1.\n\\]\nThe combined density rule has total mass one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(\\theta_v=\\pi(v+1/2)/N_{\\mathrm q}\\). The finite identity\n\\[\n\\sum_{v=0}^{N_{\\mathrm q}-1}\\cos((v+1/2)t)\n=\\frac{\\sin(N_{\\mathrm q}t)}{2\\sin(t/2)}\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\nN_{\\mathrm q}^{-1}\\sum_v\\cos(\\ell\\theta_v)=0\n\\qquad(1\\le\\ell\\le2N_{\\mathrm q}-1).\n\\]\nProduct-to-sum then gives the discrete orthogonality used in the weight formula. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\sum_vo_v\\cos(\\ell\\theta_v)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&amp;\\ell=0,\\\\\n0,&amp;\\ell\\text{ odd},\\\\\n(1-\\ell^2)^{-1},&amp;\\ell&gt;0\\text{ even},\n\\end{cases}\n\\quad0\\le\\ell\\le N_{\\mathrm q}-1.\n\\]\nThese equal \\(\\frac12\\int_{-1}^1C_\\ell(x)\\,dx\\) for the Chebyshev polynomials \\(C_\\ell(\\cos\\theta)=\\cos(\\ell\\theta)\\). Since these polynomials span all degrees through \\(383\\), exactness follows.</p><p>The telescoping identity\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=1}^{N_{\\mathrm q}/2-1}\\frac2{4a^2-1}\n=1-\\frac1{N_{\\mathrm q}-1}\n\\]\nand \\(\\cos\\le1\\) prove positivity. The case \\(\\ell=0\\) proves the weight sum. The substitution \\(t=(2j-1+x)/12\\) turns the average rule on \\([-1,1]\\) into weights \\(o_v/6\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Quadrature error for scaled moments — paper Lemma 3.1.** For one basis input among the \\(171\\) coordinates \\((\\mathcal F,e)\\), every integer \\(a\\ge0\\), every real \\(0\\le m\\le168\\), and every real \\(|y|\\le3/2\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Quadrature error for scaled moments — paper Lemma 3.1.**</p><p>For one basis input among the \\(171\\) coordinates \\((\\mathcal F,e)\\), every integer \\(a\\ge0\\), every real \\(0\\le m\\le168\\), and every real \\(|y|\\le3/2\\), replacing the density integrals by the rule of Proposition 2, with atoms unchanged, changes each of\n\\[\n\\frac{y^a}{a!}p^{(a)}(m),\\qquad\n\\frac{y^a}{a!}(Kp)^{(a)}(m)\n\\]\nby less than \\(10^{-31}\\) in absolute value.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the \\(j\\)-th density piece, put \\(u=(2j-1)/12\\) and consider \\(|t-u|\\le1/6\\). There\n\\[\n|t|\\le13/12&lt;1.1,\\quad\n|\\lambda(t)|\\le\\frac1{b(h-1/6)}&lt;5,\n\\quad\n|z(t)|\\le\\frac B{h-1/6}+h=\\frac{214}{35}&lt;6.2.\n\\]\nInversion maps the disk centered at \\(u+ih\\) of radius \\(1/6\\) to the disk with center\n\\[\n\\frac{u-ih}{u^2+h^2-1/36}\n\\]\nand radius \\((1/6)/(u^2+h^2-1/36)\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Im}z(t)\n\\ge\\frac{B(h-1/6)}{u^2+h^2-1/36}-h\n\\ge-\\frac{1402}{17505}&gt;-.081.\n\\]</p><p>Using\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell=1}^6|P_\\ell|=10,\\qquad\n\\sum_{\\ell=1}^6t_\\ell|P_\\ell|=\\frac{16}{3},\n\\]\na \\(c_n\\) density has modulus at most\n\\[\n\\frac{37\\pi^2}{3}e^{\\pi M/6}&lt;220e^{\\pi M/6};\n\\]\na \\(d_n\\) density has the smaller bound \\(20\\pi e^{\\pi M/6}\\). These include \\(n=0\\). The constant column has no density error.</p><p>The direct and transformed scaled kernel factors are bounded by\n\\[\ne^{\\pi M/6}e^{1.65\\pi},\n\\qquad\n5e^{.081\\pi M}e^{9.3\\pi},\n\\]\nbecause \\(x^a/a!\\le e^x\\). The full integrands are therefore bounded by\n\\[\n220e^{\\pi M/3+1.65\\pi},\\qquad\n1100e^{\\pi M/6+.081\\pi M+9.3\\pi}.\n\\]\nThe elementary estimates\n\\[\n\\pi&lt;22/7,\\qquad\ne&lt;\\sum_{j=0}^6\\frac1{j!}+\\frac1{7!(1-1/8)}\n=\\frac{95901}{35280}&lt;\\frac{2719}{1000}\n\\]\nmake the exponents less than \\(182,160\\), respectively. The rational comparisons\n\\[\n220(2719/1000)^{182}&lt;10^{83},\n\\qquad1100(2719/1000)^{160}&lt;10^{83}\n\\]\nbound both integrands by \\(10^{83}\\).</p><p>The real interval has half the disk radius. Cauchy's estimate bounds the Taylor tail after degree \\(383\\) by\n\\[\n10^{83}\\sum_{r=384}^\\infty2^{-r}=2\\cdot10^{83}2^{-384}.\n\\]\nExactness removes the polynomial. Integration and quadrature are positive functionals of combined mass one, so their difference on the tail is below\n\\[\n4\\cdot10^{83}2^{-384}&lt;10^{-31},\n\\]\nequivalently \\(4\\cdot10^{114}&lt;2^{384}\\). Real parts do not enlarge errors; no atom was changed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Containing parameter boxes and Bernstein range — paper Lemma 3.2 and Section 3.5.** For every finite \\(k\\in[K,J]\\), with no upper restriction if \\(J=\\infty\\), \\( lq(k)\\ge[l]_{K,J}. \\) Moreover the rows \\(L_{i,d}\\) in …", "statement": "<p>**Containing parameter boxes and Bernstein range — paper Lemma 3.2 and Section 3.5.**</p><p>For every finite \\(k\\in[K,J]\\), with no upper restriction if \\(J=\\infty\\),\n\\[\nlq(k)\\ge[l]_{K,J}.\n\\]\nMoreover the rows \\(L_{i,d}\\) in Proposition 2 are the degree-\\(40\\) Bernstein coefficients of\n\\[\n\\sum_{r=0}^{40}l_{i,r}x^r.\n\\]\nThus its values on \\(0\\le x\\le1\\) lie between its smallest and largest Bernstein coefficients.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(x_0=1/k\\) and \\(w_j=e^{-k(n_j-1)}\\) for \\(j=2,\\ldots,5\\). The physical data lie in\n\\[\nx_0\\in[1/J,1/K],\\qquad\nw_j\\in[e^{-J(n_j-1)},e^{-K(n_j-1)}].\n\\]\nThe expression\n\\[\na_1+x_0b_1+\\sum_{j=2}^5(a_j+x_0b_j)w_j\n\\]\nis affine in each variable with the others fixed. Its minimum on this containing box occurs at a vertex. Minimizing first in each \\(w_j\\), then in the shared \\(x_0\\), gives the displayed lower evaluation. This is a containing-box bound, not sampling of \\(q(k)\\).</p><p>The binomial theorem gives\n\\[\nx^r=\\sum_{d=r}^{40}\n\\frac{\\binom dr}{\\binom{40}r}\n\\binom{40}d x^d(1-x)^{40-d}.\n\\]\nThe Bernstein basis functions are nonnegative on \\([0,1]\\) and sum to one. Substitution proves the coefficient formula and range enclosure.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Conditional alternative rational verification and roundoff bounds — paper Lemma C.1 and Appendix C.** Use the arrays defined in Proposition 2 and \\(\\epsilon=10^{-80}\\).", "statement": "<p>**Conditional alternative rational verification and roundoff bounds — paper Lemma C.1 and Appendix C.**</p><p>Use the arrays defined in Proposition 2 and \\(\\epsilon=10^{-80}\\). Define componentwise floor-rounding\n\\[\nR(x)=\\epsilon\\lfloor x/\\epsilon\\rfloor\n\\]\non real numbers, separately on real and imaginary parts of complex numbers, and entrywise on matrices. For complex rational \\(w\\), set\n\\[\nu=R(w/4096),\\quad d_0=1,\\quad\nd_j=R(d_{j-1}u/j)\\quad(1\\le j\\le60),\n\\]\n\\[\nx_0=\\sum_{j=0}^{60}d_j,\\qquad\nx_{j+1}=R(x_j^2)\\quad(0\\le j&lt;12),\\qquad e_*(w)=x_{12}.\n\\]</p><p>The floor prescription is the following finite computation:</p><p class=\"li\">Replace \\(\\pi,b,\\sqrt3\\) by \\(p_*,b_*,2b_*\\), while \\(B,h\\) remain exact.</p><p class=\"li\">Evaluate cosines as real parts of \\(e_*\\), and round the resulting \\(\\tau_{jv},o_v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Form every mass entry by the formulas of Proposition 2, using \\(e_*\\) for exponentials and applying a final \\(R\\). Atom entries are also rounded.</p><p class=\"li\">At every mass point, round \\(z,\\lambda\\); put\n  \\[\n  F_0=e_*(i p_*tm),\\qquad\n  G_0=R(\\lambda e_*(i p_*zm)),\n  \\]\n  and update\n  \\[\n  F_a=R(F_{a-1}i p_*ty/a),\\qquad\n  G_a=R(G_{a-1}i p_*zy/a).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Round each projected jet entry and each nonzero entry of \\(Y\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Starting with \\(U=D^\\#\\), \\(v_+=v_-=I\\), for \\(r=0,\\ldots,8\\), use\n  \\[\n  v_+\\leftarrow v_++R(v_+U),\\qquad\n  v_-\\leftarrow v_-+R(\\varepsilon_rv_-U),\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\varepsilon_0=-1\\), \\(\\varepsilon_r=1\\) for \\(r&gt;0\\), and then, if \\(r&lt;8\\), \\(U\\leftarrow R(U^2)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Form \\(g_1^\\#,g_2^\\#\\) exactly from these projected matrices and the exact extras, and round the two final positive-node matrices in the formulas for \\(X_i\\). Append the exact extras.</p><p class=\"li\">Form all Bernstein and tail rows by ordinary exact rational products, without further floors. Their lower evaluations use \\(e_*\\) at finite endpoints and exact limiting values at infinity. For every half-gap, every function, every parameter box, and every Bernstein index, test the corresponding strict stronger cutoff in the tables in the proof of Proposition 2. Test all ten tail inequalities and every indicated power and column norm.</p><p>A superscript \\(\\#\\) denotes this rounded output.</p><p>Statements:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(|w|&lt;1700\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Re}w&lt;7\\), then\n   \\[\n   |e_*(w)-e^w|&lt;10^{-68}.\n   \\]\n   Every exponential argument in the prescription lies in this domain.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. A single-column quadrature moment of order at most \\(42\\), scaled by \\(y^a/a!\\), \\(|y|\\le3/2\\), has error below \\(10^{-48}\\). The initial operator-norm errors in \\(S_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal F}^\\#,S_{\\mathcal Fe}^\\#,Y^\\#\\) are below \\(10^{-44}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Suppose either the exact or the rounded computation has every pre-update power norm below \\(3\\), every partial-sum norm below \\(34\\), and every combined corresponding \\(X_1,X_2\\) column norm below \\(5.6\\). Then a pair of corresponding columns has combined error below \\(10^{-29}\\), every Bernstein row entry has error below \\(10^{-16}\\), and every sign lower evaluation and tail expression has error below \\(10^{-6}\\).</p><p>If the prescribed rational computation passes the stronger tables, these error bounds imply the looser exact-array conclusions of Proposition 2. This is a conditional alternative; the paper's supplied programs do not execute the full floor-rounded matrix construction.</p>", "proof": "<p>A complex componentwise floor changes a number by less than \\(2\\epsilon\\). For \\(r=w/4096\\), both \\(|r|,|u|&lt;.416\\). If \\(\\delta_j\\) bounds \\(|d_j-r^j/j!|\\), then\n\\[\n\\delta_0=0,\\qquad\n\\delta_j\\le\\frac{.416}{j}\\delta_{j-1}\n+\\frac{2\\epsilon\\, .416^{j-1}}{j!}+2\\epsilon.\n\\]\nSumming through \\(60\\), with the exponential tail, gives\n\\[\n|x_0-e^{w/4096}|\n\\le\\sum_{j=0}^{60}\\delta_j+\\frac{e^{.416}.416^{61}}{61!}\n&lt;1.263\\cdot10^{-78}&lt;4\\cdot10^{-78}.\n\\]\nThe factorial tail alone is below \\(4\\cdot10^{-107}\\). At squaring step \\(j\\), while the accumulated error is below one, the amplification is at most \\(2e^{7/2^{12-j}}+1\\). The product is bounded by \\(3^{12}e^7\\). Including twelve new floors gives\n\\[\n(4\\cdot10^{-78}+24\\epsilon)3^{12}e^7\n&lt;2.472\\cdot10^{-69}&lt;10^{-68}.\n\\]\nThis is below one at every prior stage and closes the hypothesis.</p><p>Density phases have modulus at most \\(\\pi168\\), weight phases less than \\(2\\pi191\\), and transformed phases at most\n\\[\n\\pi(44/15)168&lt;1549.\n\\]\nTransformed real parts are nonpositive and remain so after the perturbations because \\(\\operatorname{Im}z&gt;.187/\\pi\\). The only positive scalar phases are \\(K\\le6\\) at \\(m=0\\); other finite scalar phases are nonpositive and of modulus at most \\(48\\). Infinity is evaluated by limits.</p><p>The primitive error allowances are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{quantity}&amp;\\text{absolute error}\\\\ \\hline\n\\tau_{jv}&amp;10^{-67}\\\\\no_v&amp;10^{-65}/384\\\\\nz,\\lambda&amp;10^{-65}\\\\\n\\text{one density value}&amp;10^{-60}\\\\\n\\text{one mass column, summed}&amp;10^{-59}\\\\\nF_0,G_0&amp;10^{-60}\\\\\nF_a,G_a,\\ a\\le42&amp;10^{-52}\\\\\nQ_n,D_n&amp;10^{-75}.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nFor weights, replacing \\(\\pi\\) changes a phase by less than \\(382\\epsilon\\); use\n\\[\n2\\sum_{a=1}^{191}(4a^2-1)^{-1}&lt;1.\n\\]\nThe bounds\n\\[\n|z'(t)|\\le B/h^2&lt;9,\\quad\n|\\partial_t\\lambda(t)|&lt;8,\\quad\n|\\partial_b\\lambda(t)|&lt;4\n\\]\ngive the next allowances. Density phase errors are below \\(10^{-63}\\). The six-term formulas and \\(\\sum_{\\ell&gt;0}|P_\\ell|=10\\) give the density bound; positivity and total quadrature weight one control its weighted sum. Adding weight errors, all \\(2311\\) mass floors, and atomic coefficient errors gives the mass-column allowance.</p><p>Each exact mass column has summed modulus below \\(230\\): real-interval density bounds are\n\\[\n2\\pi^2\\sum_{\\ell&gt;0}t_\\ell|P_\\ell|&lt;106,\\qquad20\\pi&lt;63,\n\\]\nand constant atomic variation is \\(25\\). Zeroth phase errors are below \\(10^{-63}\\) directly and \\(6\\cdot10^{-63}\\) after transformation. Divided-power arguments have modulus below \\(15\\) and error below \\(10^{-63}\\).</p><p>For either factor \\(H_a=H_0v^a/a!\\),\n\\[\n|H_0|&lt;3,\\quad |H_0^\\#-H_0|&lt;10^{-60},\n\\quad |v|,|v^\\#|&lt;15,\\quad |v^\\#-v|&lt;10^{-63}.\n\\]\nThe initial-value, argument, and floor costs total at most\n\\[\ne^{15}(10^{-60}+3\\cdot10^{-63}+2\\epsilon)&lt;10^{-52}.\n\\]\nHere a floor introduced at step \\(j\\) has multiplier bounded by\n\\[\n15^{a-j}j!/a!\\le15^{a-j}/(a-j)!,\n\\]\nso all floors cost at most \\(2\\epsilon e^{15}\\). A moment error is thus below\n\\[\n(230+10^{-59})10^{-52}+3e^{15}10^{-59}&lt;10^{-48}.\n\\]\nJet projection uses \\(Q_n&gt;1.75\\), \\(|D_n|&lt;2.12\\), and \\(168\\) output coordinates, yielding operator errors below \\(10^{-44}\\).</p><p>A real \\(168\\)-row matrix floor costs below \\(10^{-77}\\) in norm. If \\(e_r\\) is the power error and \\(v_r\\) the partial-sum error, the assumed envelopes give\n\\[\ne_0&lt;10^{-44},\\quad e_{r+1}\\le7e_r+10^{-77},\n\\quad v_0=0,\\quad v_{r+1}\\le5v_r+34e_r+10^{-77}.\n\\]\nThese follow more precisely from square cost \\((6+e_r)e_r\\) and partial-sum coefficient \\(4+e_r\\); induction validates \\(e_r&lt;1\\). Finite rational evaluation gives\n\\[\n\\max_{0\\le r\\le8}e_r&lt;5.765\\cdot10^{-38}&lt;10^{-36},\n\\qquad v_9&lt;6.529\\cdot10^{-36}&lt;10^{-34}.\n\\]\nExchanging exact and rounded factors proves the same bounds when the envelopes are known for rounded arrays.</p><p>The combined extra-column norm is at most \\(3.3505\\), \\(\\|Y\\|&lt;2\\), and\n\\[\n2+(4000+2\\cdot10^{-27})3.3505&lt;13405.\n\\]\nThe data perturbation is below \\(10^{-43}\\), so the combined data norm is below \\(20000\\) on both computations. The two final sum-and-difference formulas, including \\(336\\) final floors, give column error\n\\[\n40000v_9+72\\cdot10^{-43}+336\\epsilon\n&lt;2.612\\cdot10^{-31}&lt;10^{-29}.\n\\]</p><p>A unit-list scaled moment is bounded by \\(230\\cdot3e^{15}\\). Since \\(|y|^{-\\nu}\\le4\\), \\(0\\le\\omega_{dr}\\le1\\), the list-error contribution to a Bernstein row entry is below\n\\[\n41\\cdot4\\cdot230\\cdot3e^{15}10^{-29}&lt;3.700\\cdot10^{-18}.\n\\]\nThe primitive-moment contribution is separately below\n\\[\n41\\cdot4\\cdot6\\cdot10^{-48}&lt;10^{-44}.\n\\]\nThus the entry error is below \\(10^{-16}\\).</p><p>Each exact Bernstein entry has modulus below \\(10^{14}\\). Each five-term lower evaluation has error below \\(2\\cdot10^{-15}\\): minima are nonexpansive in the maximum norm, and endpoint exponential errors are below \\(10^{-68}\\). Target-support additions cost below \\(27\\cdot10^{-68}\\) at \\(m=0\\), below \\(3\\cdot10^{-68}\\) at \\(m=3\\), and are exact at \\(m=1\\).</p><p>A tail row, including the sum of low \\(d_n\\) rows, has entry error below \\(10^{-29}\\) and modulus below \\(6\\). Its lower-evaluation error is below \\(2\\cdot10^{-28}\\). Taking \\(\\min(0,\\cdot)\\) does not increase it. There are \\(94\\) tail terms, each reciprocal factor at most one; hence the tail error is below \\(2\\cdot10^{-26}\\), in particular below \\(10^{-6}\\).</p><p>For implementation with integers, represent rounded complex numbers by their components multiplied by \\(S=10^{80}\\). The Taylor and squaring updates are exactly\n\\[\n(d_r,d_i)\\mapsto\n\\left(\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{d_ru_r-d_iu_i}{Sj}\\right\\rfloor,\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{d_ru_i+d_iu_r}{Sj}\\right\\rfloor\n\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\n(x_r,x_i)\\mapsto\n\\left(\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{x_r^2-x_i^2}{S}\\right\\rfloor,\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{2x_rx_i}{S}\\right\\rfloor\n\\right),\n\\]\nwith simultaneous right-side evaluation and floors toward negative infinity. The Bernstein weights can be accumulated from \\(w=1\\) by \\(w\\leftarrow w(d-r)/(40-r)\\). These are the paper's finite prescriptions, not an additional verification run.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n.00116-10^{-6}&gt;.001,\\qquad .0022-10^{-6}&gt;.0021,\n\\]\nand the column and power error allowances are smaller than the gaps between the stronger and looser cutoffs. Therefore successful rational tests imply Proposition 2.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The following strict bounds hold:", "statement": "<p>**Scalar budgets for interpolation and signs — paper Appendix A.**</p><p>Definitions:\nRetain \\(U_r,z,t_j\\) from Proposition 1 and put\n\\[\nc_{\\mathrm{jet}}\n=\\frac{1+(1+2.12)/2.36}{1.75}\n=\\frac{548}{413},\n\\qquad \\rho=e^{-2.36},\n\\qquad s_0=89.5.\n\\]\nFor \\(m,Y,\\nu\\) in the table below, set\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal R(m,Y,\\nu)\n={}&amp;\\frac{Y^{-\\nu}36\\cdot5}\n{(41+\\nu)!\\,[1-3\\pi Y/(42+\\nu)]}\\\\\n&amp;\\times\\left[\n(\\pi Y)^{41+\\nu}\n+3\\sum_{j=1}^6e^{-\\pi\\operatorname{Im}z(t_j)m}\n(\\pi|z(t_{j-1})|Y)^{41+\\nu}\\right].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following strict bounds hold:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{quantity}&amp;\\text{upper bound}\\\\ \\hline\nU_{168}(1,0)&amp;1.0670\\cdot10^{-14}\\\\\nU_{168}(5,.02)&amp;8.1575\\cdot10^{-14}&lt;8.3\\cdot10^{-14}-10^{-170}\\\\\nU_{91}(5,.02)&amp;2.2711\\cdot10^{-7}\\\\\nc_{\\mathrm{jet}}e^{-2.36\\cdot11}/(1-e^{-2.36})&amp;7.7915\\cdot10^{-12}\\\\\n(2.3+5.6/2.36)\\sum_{n\\in\\{1,3,4,7,9\\}}e^{-2.36(n-1)}&amp;4.7185\\\\\n(36/2)(2\\cdot10^{-8})\\,3(3\\pi)^2&amp;9.5933\\cdot10^{-5}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nc_{\\mathrm{jet}}\\frac{\\rho^{168}}{1-\\rho}&lt;10^{-170},\n\\quad\nc_{\\mathrm{jet}}\\frac{\\rho^{90}}{1-\\rho}&lt;10^{-89},\n\\quad\nc_{\\mathrm{jet}}(1+\\rho^2+\\rho^3+\\rho^6+\\rho^8)&lt;1.35.\n\\]\nFurthermore,\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n(m,Y,\\nu)&amp;\\mathcal R(m,Y,\\nu)\\text{ is below}\\\\ \\hline\n(0,1/2,0)&amp;1.8\\cdot10^{-19}\\\\\n(1,1,2)&amp;10^{-11}\\\\\n(3,1,2)&amp;3\\cdot10^{-16}\\\\\n(4,3/2,2)&amp;2\\cdot10^{-11}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{e^6}{6}\\frac{3^{41}}{41!(1-3/42)}\n&lt;8\\cdot10^{-29},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{6\\cdot6^{41}}{43!(1-6/44)}&lt;10^{-20}.\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;36\\pi^2(2.4\\cdot10^{-7})\n+3(3\\pi)^2\\,36\\cdot5\\,e^{-.70s_0}\\\\\n&amp;\\quad+1.25(1.32\\pi)^2\n\\left(\\frac{2.54\\cdot5}{s_0}+.04\\right)e^{-.187s_0}\n+2.36e^{-2.36(s_0-1)}\n&lt;8.5485\\cdot10^{-5}&lt;.0002,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nand \\(12-8\\sqrt2&gt;.6862&gt;.68\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For rational \\(a\\ge0\\), define \\(P_q(a)=\\sum_{j=0}^qa^j/j!\\). If \\(a&gt;0\\) and \\(q+2&gt;a\\), the positive tail satisfies\n\\[\nP_q(a)&lt;e^a&lt;\nP_q(a)+\\frac{a^{q+1}}{(q+1)!}\\frac1{1-a/(q+2)}.\n\\]\nReciprocation gives the corresponding bounds for \\(e^{-a}\\). At \\(a=0\\), use equality.</p><p>For the tabulated \\(U_r\\), reference norm, omitted jets, quotient correction, half-gap remainders, target-support tails, tail derivative, and product endpoint, the paper's rational route uses \\(q=2000\\), the \\(\\pi\\)-bracket of Lemma 1.1, and rational square-root brackets of width \\(10^{-80}\\). A root of \\(A_{\\mathrm{num}}/A_{\\mathrm{den}}\\) is bracketed by finding an integer \\(m\\) such that\n\\[\nm^2A_{\\mathrm{den}}\\le10^{160}A_{\\mathrm{num}}\n&lt;(m+1)^2A_{\\mathrm{den}}.\n\\]\nFor the remainders,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Im}z(t)=\\frac{Bh}{t^2+h^2}-h,\\qquad\n|z(t)|^2=h^2+\\frac{B(B-2h^2)}{t^2+h^2}.\n\\]\nThus the relevant radicands are positive rationals; those used are between one and nine. All exponential magnitudes in this scalar list are below \\(250\\): the largest explicit target phase is \\(208.86\\), the largest tail phase \\(117.6\\), and half-gap phases are below \\(38\\). Finite rational evaluation gives the printed outward margins. The supplied \\(\\texttt{arithmetic\\_bounds.py}\\) instead checks these scalar budgets and Appendix C propagation majorants using \\(512\\)-bit Arb balls, exact rational arithmetic, and exact integer samples of the rounded exponential routine; it does not implement the degree-\\(2000\\) route.</p><p>For the distant target tails, a shorter local calculation suffices:\n\\[\nP_7(59/25)&gt;\n1+2.36+2.78+2.19+1.29+.61+.239+.08\n=10.549&gt;21/2.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\rho&lt;2/21\\). Also\n\\[\n\\frac{548}{413}\\frac{21}{19}&lt;\\frac32,\n\\qquad\n(21/20)^{168}&gt;(21/20)^{160}&gt;(3/2)^{16}&gt;150.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nc_{\\mathrm{jet}}\\frac{\\rho^{168}}{1-\\rho}\n&lt;\\frac32\\frac{10^{-168}}{150}=10^{-170}.\n\\]\nThe same estimate at exponent \\(90\\) gives a bound below \\(\\frac32\\,10^{-90}&lt;10^{-89}\\). It also gives the displayed first-five-node estimate.</p><p>The included-moment quadrature cost used with the remainder table is\n\\[\n41\\cdot4\\cdot5\\cdot10^{-31}=8.2\\cdot10^{-29}.\n\\]\nAdding it to each listed remainder remains below \\(10^{-8}\\).</p><p>The remaining interpolation arithmetic is\n\\[\n\\frac{36}{1-.004^8}&lt;37,\\qquad\n\\frac{37}{1-37(2\\cdot10^{-27})}&lt;40,\n\\]\n\\[\n(1.1\\cdot10^{-14})(1+40\\cdot4000)=1.760011\\cdot10^{-9}&lt;1,\n\\]\n\\[\n20000(.004)^8=1.31072\\cdot10^{-15}&lt;1.4\\cdot10^{-15}.\n\\]\nThe finite residual budget is\n\\[\n8\\cdot10^{-12}+10^{-24}+1.4\\cdot10^{-15}&lt;10^{-11}.\n\\]\nIf\n\\[\nu_{\\mathcal F}\\le40(10^{-11}+4000u_{\\mathcal T}),\\qquad\nu_{\\mathcal T}\\le8.3\\cdot10^{-14}\n+(1.1\\cdot10^{-14})(u_{\\mathcal F}+u_{\\mathcal T}),\n\\]\ntheir rational solution gives\n\\[\nu_{\\mathcal F}+u_{\\mathcal T}&lt;1.3681\\cdot10^{-8}&lt;2\\cdot10^{-8}.\n\\]\nThe final sign arithmetic is\n\\[\n.001-.00011-.000001-.000001=.000888&gt;0,\n\\]\n\\[\n.0021-2\\cdot10^{-8}&gt;.002,\\qquad .68\\cdot.002&gt;.0001.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a unique pair of positive-node lists in \\(\\mathcal E\\), with the fixed extras, such that \\( H_1(n)=T_k(n),\\quad H_1'(n)=T_k'(n),\\quad H_2(n)=H_2'(n)=0\\qquad(n\\in\\mathcal N). \\) They satisfy …", "statement": "<p>**Exact interpolation — paper Proposition 4.1.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Retain Propositions 1–3, let \\(k\\ge2.36\\) be real, and put\n  \\[\n  T_k(s)=k^{-1}e^{-k(s-1)}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal E=\\ell^1(\\mathcal N;\\mathbb R^2),\\qquad\n  \\mathcal T=\\mathcal N\\setminus\\mathcal F.\n  \\]\n  Pair norms are sums of the two list norms. Fix extras \\(e_i=E_iq(k)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For full lists \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\), put\n  \\[\n  H_1=p_{\\ell_1}+Kp_{\\ell_2},\\qquad\n  H_2=p_{\\ell_2}+Kp_{\\ell_1}.\n  \\]\n  Define reference lists \\(\\ell_i^{\\mathrm{ref}}=X_iq(k)\\), extended by zero on \\(\\mathcal T\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a unique pair of positive-node lists in \\(\\mathcal E\\), with the fixed extras, such that\n\\[\nH_1(n)=T_k(n),\\quad H_1'(n)=T_k'(n),\\quad\nH_2(n)=H_2'(n)=0\\qquad(n\\in\\mathcal N).\n\\]\nThey satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\|\\ell_i^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\|_1&lt;4.8,\\qquad\n\\sum_i\\|\\ell_i-\\ell_i^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\|_1&lt;2\\cdot10^{-8},\n\\qquad\n\\sum_i\\|\\ell_i\\|_1&lt;5.\n\\]\nTheir \\(H_1,H_2\\) are entire, real on the real axis, and give the Schwartz Fourier pair of Proposition 1. In particular, \\(H_1-T_k,H_2\\) have zeros of order at least two at every positive node, including artificial nodes. Uniqueness is only in this summable coefficient system with the specified extras and enlarged node set.</p>", "proof": "<p>The jet identities of Proposition 1 make the required equations exactly\n\\[\n\\mathscr L\\binom{a_1}{a_2}\n=\\binom{\\mathcal JT_k+S_ee_2}{S_ee_1}.\n\\]\nLemma 4.1 proves existence and uniqueness with fixed extras.</p><p>For the correction, put\n\\[\nu_{\\mathcal F}=\\|(a-a^{\\mathrm{ref}})_{\\mathcal F}\\|_1,\\qquad\nu_{\\mathcal T}=\\|(a-a^{\\mathrm{ref}})_{\\mathcal T}\\|_1.\n\\]\nThe block equations and Lemmas 4.1–4.2 give\n\\[\nu_{\\mathcal F}\\le40(10^{-11}+4000u_{\\mathcal T}),\n\\]\n\\[\nu_{\\mathcal T}\\le8.3\\cdot10^{-14}\n+1.1\\cdot10^{-14}(u_{\\mathcal F}+u_{\\mathcal T}).\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\nu_{\\mathcal T}&lt;8.31\\cdot10^{-14},\\qquad\nu_{\\mathcal F}&lt;1.37\\cdot10^{-8},\n\\]\nand their sum is below \\(2\\cdot10^{-8}\\). Extras did not change, so this is the full-list correction. Adding the reference bound yields combined exact norm below \\(5\\).</p><p>The solved jet equations recover values and first derivatives at every positive node. Entirety, reality, and the Schwartz Fourier pair follow from Proposition 1.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Invertibility of the coupled jet operator — paper Proposition 4.1, first part.** On \\(\\mathcal E^2\\), define \\( \\mathscr L= \\begin{pmatrix}I&-S\\\\-S&I\\end{pmatrix}, \\) where \\(S\\) here restricts to positive-node inputs.", "statement": "<p>**Invertibility of the coupled jet operator — paper Proposition 4.1, first part.**</p><p>On \\(\\mathcal E^2\\), define\n\\[\n\\mathscr L=\n\\begin{pmatrix}I&amp;-S\\\\-S&amp;I\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nwhere \\(S\\) here restricts to positive-node inputs. Then \\(\\mathscr L\\) is boundedly invertible. Its exact finite block satisfies\n\\[\n\\|\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal F}^{-1}\\|&lt;40.\n\\]\nWith \\(\\delta=1.1\\cdot10^{-14}\\),\n\\[\n\\|\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal T\\mathcal F}\\|&lt;\\delta,\\quad\n\\|\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal T\\mathcal T}-I\\|&lt;\\delta,\\quad\n\\|\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal T}\\|&lt;4000.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The target belongs to \\(\\mathcal E\\), since\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{T_k(n)}{Q_n}\\right|\n+\\left|\\frac{T_k'(n)-D_nT_k(n)}{Q_n}\\right|\n\\le\\frac{1+(1+2.12)/k}{1.75}e^{-k(n-1)}.\n\\]\nThe extras and \\(S\\) are bounded by Proposition 1.</p><p>For the quadrature finite block\n\\[\n\\mathscr L_D=\\begin{pmatrix}I&amp;-D\\\\-D&amp;I\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nput \\(\\eta=.004^8\\). Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\n\\|D^{512}\\|=\\|(D^{64})^8\\|&lt;\\eta.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nR_+=(I-D)^{-1}=V_+(I-D^{512})^{-1},\n\\quad\nR_-=(I+D)^{-1}=V_-(I-D^{512})^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe final inverses are norm-convergent Neumann series. Sum-and-difference diagonalization gives\n\\[\n\\mathscr L_D^{-1}\n=\\frac12\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nR_++R_-&amp;R_+-R_-\\\\\nR_+-R_-&amp;R_++R_-\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n\\|\\mathscr L_D^{-1}\\|\n\\le\\|R_+\\|+\\|R_-\\|\n&lt;\\frac{36}{1-\\eta}&lt;37.\n\\]</p><p>Apply Lemma 2.2 at orders zero and one with \\(y=1\\). Jet projection makes each entry of\n\\(S_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal F}-D\\), and of\n\\(S_{\\mathcal Fe}-\\widetilde S_{\\mathcal Fe}\\), smaller than \\(10^{-29}\\). There are \\(168\\) output coordinates; the paired block is off-diagonal, so no extra factor two occurs:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal F}-\\mathscr L_D\\|&lt;2\\cdot10^{-27}.\n\\]\nA second Neumann series gives the bound \\(40\\).</p><p>Propositions 1 and 3 give \\(U_{168}(1,0)&lt;\\delta\\), uniformly in input locations, yielding the two small tail-output bounds. The remaining block has norm below \\(4000\\). Eliminate the finite unknowns. The Schur complement\n\\[\n\\mathscr Q=\n\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal T\\mathcal T}\n-\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal T\\mathcal F}\n\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal F}^{-1}\n\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal T}\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\|\\mathscr Q-I\\|&lt;\\delta(1+40\\cdot4000)\n=1.760011\\cdot10^{-9}&lt;1.\n\\]\nFor any right side \\(\\psi\\),\n\\[\na_{\\mathcal T}\n=\\mathscr Q^{-1}\n(\\psi_{\\mathcal T}-\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal T\\mathcal F}\n\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal F}^{-1}\\psi_{\\mathcal F}),\n\\]\n\\[\na_{\\mathcal F}\n=\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal F}^{-1}\n(\\psi_{\\mathcal F}-\\mathscr L_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal T}a_{\\mathcal T}).\n\\]\nAll operators are bounded, proving full-space invertibility, not merely invertibility of a truncation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Reference size and interpolation residual — paper Proposition 4.1, remaining estimates.** With the fixed extras and reference lists of Proposition 4, let \\(a^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\) be their paired positive-node portion, and …", "statement": "<p>**Reference size and interpolation residual — paper Proposition 4.1, remaining estimates.**</p><p>With the fixed extras and reference lists of Proposition 4, let \\(a^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\) be their paired positive-node portion, and let\n\\[\nr=\n\\binom{\\mathcal JT_k+S_ee_2}{S_ee_1}\n-\\mathscr L a^{\\mathrm{ref}},\n\\]\nwhere \\(S_e\\) is the restriction of \\(S\\) to extras. Then\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\|\\ell_i^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\|_1&lt;4.8,\\qquad\n|C_1|+|C_2|&lt;.02,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|r_{\\mathcal F}\\|_1&lt;10^{-11},\\qquad\n\\|r_{\\mathcal T}\\|_1&lt;8.3\\cdot10^{-14}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The same-column bounds of Proposition 2 and nonnegativity of \\(q(k)\\) give\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\|\\ell_i^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\|_1\n\\le(2.3+5.6/k)\n(1+e^{-2k}+e^{-3k}+e^{-6k}+e^{-8k})&lt;4.8\n\\]\nby Proposition 3. The constants are fixed:\n\\[\n|C_1|+|C_2|\n=|-.0074+.0115/k|+|.0096-.017/k|\n=.017-.0285/k&lt;.02.\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(\\gamma_i=g_iq(k)\\), and\n\\[\n\\Delta S_{\\mathcal F}\n=(S_{\\mathcal F\\mathcal F}-D,\\\nS_{\\mathcal Fe}-\\widetilde S_{\\mathcal Fe}).\n\\]\nThe geometric identities give the exact residual decomposition\n\\[\nr_{\\mathcal F}\n=\n\\binom{\\mathcal J_{\\mathcal F}T_k-Yq(k)}0\n+\\binom{\\Delta S_{\\mathcal F}\\ell_2^{\\mathrm{ref}}}\n{\\Delta S_{\\mathcal F}\\ell_1^{\\mathrm{ref}}}\n+\\binom{D^{512}\\gamma_1}{D^{512}\\gamma_2}.\n\\]\nThe omitted target nodes begin at \\(12\\); their cost is at most\n\\[\nc_{\\mathrm{jet}}\\sum_{n=12}^\\infty e^{-2.36(n-1)}&lt;8\\cdot10^{-12}.\n\\]\nQuadrature costs less than\n\\[\n168\\cdot10^{-29}\\cdot4.8&lt;10^{-24}.\n\\]\nThe first-five target norm is below \\(1.35\\), and the extra-input operator norm below \\(4000+2\\cdot10^{-27}\\), so\n\\[\n\\|\\gamma_1\\|_1+\\|\\gamma_2\\|_1\n&lt;1.35+(4000+2\\cdot10^{-27})4.8&lt;20000.\n\\]\nThe geometric residual is below \\(20000(.004)^8&lt;1.4\\cdot10^{-15}\\). Summing proves the finite bound.</p><p>On \\(\\mathcal T\\), reference positive-node coefficients vanish. Their transformed outputs, including extras, cost at most \\(U_{168}(5,.02)\\). The target tail costs below \\(10^{-170}\\). Proposition 3 retains the separate margin\n\\[\nU_{168}(5,.02)+10^{-170}&lt;8.3\\cdot10^{-14},\n\\]\nwhich proves the tail bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Normalized Gaussian Fourier pair — paper Theorem 2.1, an intermediate auxiliary theorem.** For every finite real \\(k\\ge2.36\\), put \\( T_k(s)=k^{-1}e^{-k(s-1)}. \\) There are entire functions \\(H_1,H_2\\), real on the …", "statement": "<p>**Normalized Gaussian Fourier pair — paper Theorem 2.1, an intermediate auxiliary theorem.**</p><p>For every finite real \\(k\\ge2.36\\), put\n\\[\nT_k(s)=k^{-1}e^{-k(s-1)}.\n\\]\nThere are entire functions \\(H_1,H_2\\), real on the real axis, and a real radial Schwartz function \\(f\\) on \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) such that\n\\[\nf(x)=e^{-\\pi hb|x|^2}H_1(b|x|^2),\n\\qquad\n\\widehat f(\\xi)=e^{-\\pi hb|\\xi|^2}H_2(b|\\xi|^2),\n\\]\nand, for every real \\(s\\ge0\\),\n\\[\nH_1(s)\\le T_k(s),\\qquad H_2(s)\\ge0.\n\\]\nAt every \\(n\\in\\mathcal N\\),\n\\[\nH_1(n)=T_k(n),\\qquad H_1'(n)=-e^{-k(n-1)},\n\\qquad H_2(n)=H_2'(n)=0.\n\\]\nThe full parameter range and every artificial node are part of the assertion.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 4 supplies summable real lists with the prescribed exact jets. Proposition 1 gives entire real profiles and the genuine radial Schwartz Fourier pair. Lemmas 5.2 and 5.4 cover all \\(s\\ge0\\), proving \\(T_k-H_1\\ge0\\) and \\(H_2\\ge0\\). The jets are those already established in Proposition 4. This proves the paper's normalized auxiliary theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Exponential supports — paper Lemma 5.1.** For an entire \\(u\\), define \\( \\mathcal D_{m,2}u(s) =\\frac{u(s)-u(m)-(s-m)u'(m)}{(s-m)^2}, \\qquad \\mathcal D_{m,2}u(m)=u''(m)/2, \\) and \\(\\mathcal D_{0,0}u=u\\).", "statement": "<p>**Exponential supports — paper Lemma 5.1.**</p><p>For an entire \\(u\\), define\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{m,2}u(s)\n=\\frac{u(s)-u(m)-(s-m)u'(m)}{(s-m)^2},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal D_{m,2}u(m)=u''(m)/2,\n\\]\nand \\(\\mathcal D_{0,0}u=u\\). On any prescribed half-gap \\(s=m+yx\\), \\(0\\le x\\le1\\), use \\(\\nu=0\\) at zero and \\(\\nu=2\\) otherwise.</p><p>For consecutive parameter endpoints \\(K,J\\), finite \\(k\\ge K\\), and \\(k\\le J\\) when \\(J&lt;\\infty\\), the coefficients \\(a_r^*\\) of Proposition 2 satisfy\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{m,\\nu}T_k(m+yx)\n\\ge\\sum_{r=0}^{40}a_r^*x^r-.000001.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At the origin, \\(0\\le s\\le1/2\\), and\n\\[\n\\partial_kT_k(s)=T_k(s)(1-s-1/k)&gt;0\n\\]\nbecause \\(k\\ge2.36&gt;2\\). Replacing \\(k\\) by \\(K\\) lowers the target, whose Taylor coefficients are the first case of \\(a_r^*\\).</p><p>At node \\(1\\), write \\(u=s-1\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{1,2}T_k(1+u)\n=\\frac{e^{-ku}-1+ku}{ku^2},\n\\]\nwith removable value \\(k/2\\). Its \\(k\\)-derivative for \\(u\\ne0\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac{1-(1+ku)e^{-ku}}{k^2u^2}\\ge0\n\\]\nby \\(e^{ku}\\ge1+ku\\). This works for either signed half-gap. Replacing \\(k\\) by \\(K\\) gives coefficients \\(K(-Ky)^r/(r+2)!\\).</p><p>Every left endpoint satisfies \\(K\\le6\\), including the unbounded box. At zero \\(K|y|\\le3\\), and at \\(1\\), \\(K|y|\\le6\\). Proposition 3 bounds the respective degree-\\(40\\) tails by \\(8\\cdot10^{-29}\\) and \\(10^{-20}\\), both below \\(10^{-6}\\).</p><p>At any positive center, Taylor's integral formula gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{m,2}T_k(m+u)\n=k\\int_0^1(1-v)e^{-k(m-1+vu)}\\,dv\\ge0.\n\\]\nThis handles every zero-support case. At \\(m=3\\), \\(-1\\le u\\le1/2\\), so \\(a=2+vu\\ge1\\), and \\(ke^{-ak}\\) decreases for \\(k\\ge2.36\\). For finite \\(J\\), the quotient is at least\n\\[\nJ\\int_0^1(1-v)e^{-J(2+vu)}\\,dv.\n\\]\nThe density \\(2(1-v)\\) has mean \\(1/3\\). Jensen's inequality bounds this below by\n\\[\n\\frac J2e^{-J(2+u/3)}\\ge\\frac J2e^{-13J/6}.\n\\]\nFor \\(J=\\infty\\), use the nonnegative support.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Signs on finite half-gaps — paper Lemma 5.2.** Take the exact lists from Proposition 4 and put \\( F_i(s)=\\sigma_iH_i(s)+\\delta_iT_k(s), \\quad(\\sigma_1,\\sigma_2)=(-1,1),\\quad \\delta_i=\\mathbf1_{i=1}. \\) Then …", "statement": "<p>**Signs on finite half-gaps — paper Lemma 5.2.**</p><p>Take the exact lists from Proposition 4 and put\n\\[\nF_i(s)=\\sigma_iH_i(s)+\\delta_iT_k(s),\n\\quad(\\sigma_1,\\sigma_2)=(-1,1),\\quad \\delta_i=\\mathbf1_{i=1}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nF_i(s)\\ge0\\qquad(0\\le s\\le s_0=89.5,\\ i=1,2).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Exact interpolation gives \\(F_i(m)=F_i'(m)=0\\) at every positive node. Therefore\n\\[\nF_i(s)=(s-m)^2\\mathcal D_{m,2}F_i(s)\n\\]\nthere, with no division of a nonzero interpolation error.</p><p>For a twice continuously differentiable \\(u\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{m,2}u(s)\n=\\int_0^1(1-v)u''(m+v(s-m))\\,dv.\n\\]\nLet \\(H_i^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\) use the exact spectral integrals for the reference lists. Proposition 4, spectral variation at most \\(36\\) per unit list, and Proposition 1 give\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\text{half-gap}}\n|\\mathcal D_{m,\\nu}(H_i-H_i^{\\mathrm{ref}})|\n\\le\\frac{36}{\\nu!}(2\\cdot10^{-8})\n\\max\\{\\pi^\\nu,3(3\\pi)^\\nu\\}&lt;.00011.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\nu=2\\), the factor \\(1/2\\) is \\(\\int_0^1(1-v)\\,dv\\); no zero jet is required of the reference function.</p><p>Taylor expansion after deleting values and slopes gives\n\\[\n\\sigma_i\\mathcal D_{m,\\nu}H_i^{\\mathrm{ref}}(m+yx)\n=\\sum_{r=0}^\\infty\n\\frac{\\sigma_i y^r}{(r+\\nu)!}\n(H_i^{\\mathrm{ref}})^{(r+\\nu)}(m)x^r.\n\\]\nFor the first \\(41\\) coefficients, Lemma 2.2 and \\(|y|^{-\\nu}\\le4\\) give total quadrature cost at most \\(41\\cdot4\\cdot5\\cdot10^{-31}\\).</p><p>For omitted powers put \\(Y=|y|\\), \\(\\ell=41+\\nu\\). Their total is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{Y^{-\\nu}36\\cdot5}{\\ell![1-3\\pi Y/(\\ell+1)]}\n\\left[\n(\\pi Y)^\\ell+\n3\\max_{1\\le j\\le6}\ne^{-\\pi\\operatorname{Im}z(t_j)m}\n(\\pi|z(t_{j-1})|Y)^\\ell\n\\right].\n\\]\nOn each frequency interval, \\(\\operatorname{Im}z\\) and \\(|z|\\) decrease, so the opposite endpoints give the indicated safe factors. The ratio of successive absolute exponential-series terms is at most \\(3\\pi Y/(\\ell+1)&lt;1\\). Proposition 3 replaces the maximum by a larger sum and bounds the four covering cases\n\\[\n(0,1/2,0),\\quad(1,1,2),\\quad(3,1,2),\\quad(4,3/2,2).\n\\]\nThe bound decreases when \\(m\\) increases or \\(Y\\) decreases. Thus\n\\[\n\\sup_{0\\le x\\le1}\n\\left|\\sigma_i\\mathcal D_{m,\\nu}H_i^{\\mathrm{ref}}(m+yx)\n-\\sum_{r=0}^{40}l_{i,r}q(k)x^r\\right|&lt;.000001.\n\\]</p><p>Combine the finite polynomial and target support:\n\\[\n\\Phi_i(x;k)=\\sum_{r=0}^{40}(l_{i,r}q(k)+\\delta_i a_r^*)x^r.\n\\]\nIts Bernstein coefficients are\n\\[\nL_{i,d}q(k)+\\delta_i\\sum_{r=0}^d\\omega_{dr}a_r^*.\n\\]\nProposition 2 and the containing-box bound make every coefficient greater than \\(.001\\). Thus \\(\\Phi_i&gt;.001\\) on \\([0,1]\\). Lemma 5.1 and the two error estimates give\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{m,\\nu}F_i(m+yx)\n&gt;.001-.00011-.000001-.000001=.000888&gt;0.\n\\]\nAt zero this is the desired sign; at a positive center multiply by \\((s-m)^2\\) and use the exact zero at the center. The \\(91\\) half-gaps cover \\([0,89.5]\\), and the parameter boxes cover all finite \\(k\\ge2.36\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Quadratic barrier for the sine product — paper Lemma 5.3.** For real \\(s\\), choose any nearest point \\(m\\in\\mathcal Z\\).", "statement": "<p>**Quadratic barrier for the sine product — paper Lemma 5.3.**</p><p>For real \\(s\\), choose any nearest point \\(m\\in\\mathcal Z\\). Then\n\\[\nP(s)\\ge.68(s-m)^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On a half-gap from \\(m\\) to an adjacent midpoint, the quotient \\(P(s)/(s-m)^2\\) is positive after removal at the center, and its logarithm is concave. For the vanishing factor, with \\(u=s-m\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{d^2}{du^2}\\log\\frac{\\sin^2(\\pi u/12)}{u^2}\n=\\frac2{u^2}-2\\left(\\frac\\pi{12}\\right)^2\n\\csc^2(\\pi u/12)\\le0\n\\]\nby \\(|\\sin x|\\le|x|\\). Every other sine factor also has nonpositive logarithmic second derivative. Thus the quotient is bounded below by the smaller endpoint value.</p><p>At the center the value is \\(Q_m&gt;1.75\\). At the midpoints, direct product-to-sum calculation gives\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{gap modulo }12&amp; P(s)/(s-m)^2\\\\ \\hline\n[0,1],[3,4]&amp;12-8\\sqrt2\\\\{}\n[1,3]&amp;1\\\\{}\n[4,7],[9,12]&amp;\\frac89(3+\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3+\\sqrt6)\\\\{}\n[7,9]&amp;9.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nFor the smallest entry, at \\(s=1/2\\), \\(\\theta=\\pi/24\\), pair residues \\((1,7),(3,9),(0,4)\\):\n\\[\n(2\\sin(-\\theta))(2\\sin(-13\\theta))=2\\sin(\\pi/12),\n\\]\n\\[\n(2\\sin(-5\\theta))(2\\sin(-17\\theta))=2\\sin(5\\pi/12),\n\\]\n\\[\n(2\\sin\\theta)(2\\sin(-7\\theta))=1-\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nThe first two right sides multiply to one, so the quotient is\n\\[\n4(1-\\sqrt2)^2=12-8\\sqrt2&gt;.6862&gt;.68.\n\\]\nThe symmetry \\(P(4-s)=P(s)\\), from invariance of \\(L\\) under \\(a\\mapsto4-a\\bmod12\\), accounts for paired gaps. All other endpoint values are larger. The half-gaps cover the real line.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Signs in the tail — paper Lemma 5.4.** For the exact slacks \\(F_i\\) of Lemma 5.2, \\( F_i(s)\\ge0\\qquad(s\\ge s_0=89.5,\\ i=1,2). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Signs in the tail — paper Lemma 5.4.**</p><p>For the exact slacks \\(F_i\\) of Lemma 5.2,\n\\[\nF_i(s)\\ge0\\qquad(s\\ge s_0=89.5,\\ i=1,2).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Separate the direct profiles as\n\\[\nR_{i,0}(s)=C_i+\\sum_{\\substack{n\\in\\mathcal N_0\\\\n\\le88}}\n\\left(\\frac{c_{in}}{(s-n)^2}+\\frac{d_{in}}{s-n}\\right),\n\\qquad\np_i=P R_{i,0}+p_{i,\\mathrm{high}}.\n\\]\nThe high profile starts at node \\(91\\) and has no constant column.</p><p>For the reference lists, group simple poles using\n\\[\n(s-n)^{-1}=s^{-1}+n/[s(s-n)].\n\\]\nEach numerator row has lower bound \\(\\eta_{K,J}(l)\\le0\\); its positive reciprocal multiplier decreases with \\(s\\). The tail certificate in Proposition 2 therefore gives\n\\[\n\\sigma_iR_{i,0}^{\\mathrm{ref}}(s)&gt;.0021\\qquad(s\\ge s_0).\n\\]\nSince \\(s-n\\ge1.5\\) at low nodes, all coefficient multipliers have modulus at most one. The correction costs below \\(2\\cdot10^{-8}\\), giving\n\\[\n\\sigma_iR_{i,0}(s)&gt;.002.\n\\]</p><p>Let\n\\[\nw=\\sum_{i,n}(|c_{in}|+|d_{in}|),\\qquad c=|C_1|+|C_2|.\n\\]\nThen \\(w+c&lt;5\\), \\(c&lt;.02\\). At \\(n\\ge91\\), exact interpolation gives\n\\[\n(c_{1n},d_{1n})=(S\\ell_2)_n+(\\mathcal JT_k)_n,\\qquad\n(c_{2n},d_{2n})=(S\\ell_1)_n.\n\\]\nThe summed high-list norm is at most \\(U_{91}(w,c)\\) plus target cost below \\(10^{-89}\\), hence\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\sum_{n\\ge91}(|c_{in}|+|d_{in}|)\n&lt;2.2711\\cdot10^{-7}+10^{-89}&lt;2.4\\cdot10^{-7}.\n\\]</p><p>Define\n\\[\nZ_i=\\sigma_i(p_{i,\\mathrm{high}}+Kp_{3-i})+\\delta_iT_k.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nF_i=\\sigma_iP R_{i,0}+Z_i.\n\\]\nThe direct high contribution to \\(|Z_i''|\\) is at most \\(36\\pi^2(2.4\\cdot10^{-7})\\). For transformed low frequencies,\n\\[\n25c+36w\\le36(c+w)&lt;180.\n\\]\nFor the last frequency interval use \\(w&lt;5,c&lt;.02\\). Also\n\\[\nT_k''(s)=ke^{-k(s-1)}\n\\]\nhas its supremum on \\(s\\ge s_0,k\\ge2.36\\) at \\(s=s_0,k=2.36\\). Proposition 3's four-term bound proves\n\\[\n\\sup_{s\\ge s_0}|Z_i''(s)|&lt;.0002.\n\\]</p><p>At each node \\(m\\ge91\\), \\(R_{i,0}\\) is regular and \\(P\\) has a double zero; subtracting its product from the exact slack jets gives\n\\[\nZ_i(m)=Z_i'(m)=0.\n\\]\nFor any \\(s\\ge s_0\\), choose a nearest node \\(m\\ge91\\); at \\(s_0\\), choose \\(91\\) from the tie with \\(88\\). The connecting segment remains in the tail. Taylor's formula yields\n\\[\n|Z_i(s)|\\le.0001(s-m)^2.\n\\]\nLemma 5.3 and the low rational margin give\n\\[\n\\sigma_iP(s)R_{i,0}(s)\\ge.68\\cdot.002(s-m)^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(.68\\cdot.002&gt;.0001\\), the slack is nonnegative. At a node, use its exact zero without division.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Linear programming with centered disk density — paper Proposition 6.1.** Let \\(f\\) be a real even Schwartz function on \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) with \\(\\widehat f\\ge0\\).", "statement": "<p>**Linear programming with centered disk density — paper Proposition 6.1.**</p><p>Let \\(f\\) be a real even Schwartz function on \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) with \\(\\widehat f\\ge0\\). If \\(C\\) is locally finite with \\(N_R/(\\pi R^2)\\to1\\), then\n\\[\n\\liminf_{R\\to\\infty}\\frac1{N_R}\n\\sum_{\\substack{x,y\\in C_R\\\\x\\ne y}}f(x-y)\n\\ge\\widehat f(0)-f(0).\n\\]\nThe same bound holds when \\(f(x-y)\\) is replaced by a nonnegative\n\\(\\Phi:\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{0\\}\\to[0,\\infty)\\) satisfying \\(\\Phi\\ge f\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For finite real signed measures define\n\\[\nQ(\\mu,\\nu)=\\iint f(x-y)\\,d\\mu(x)\\,d\\nu(y).\n\\]\nFourier inversion and Fubini give\n\\[\nQ(\\mu,\\nu)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\n\\widehat f(\\xi)\\overline{\\widehat\\mu(\\xi)}\\widehat\\nu(\\xi)\\,d\\xi.\n\\]\nThe transforms of finite measures are bounded and \\(\\widehat f\\) is integrable. Since \\(\\widehat f\\ge0\\), \\(Q\\) is positive semidefinite and satisfies Cauchy–Schwarz.</p><p>Put \\(a=\\widehat f(0)=\\int f\\ge0\\) and\n\\(\\mu_R=\\sum_{x\\in C_R}\\delta_x\\). If \\(a=0\\), positivity gives \\(Q(\\mu_R,\\mu_R)/N_R\\ge0=a\\). Suppose \\(a&gt;0\\). Fix \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and set\n\\[\n\\nu_R=\\mathbf1_{B_{(1+\\varepsilon)R}}\\,dx,\\qquad\nV_R=\\pi(1+\\varepsilon)^2R^2.\n\\]\nFor every \\(x\\in B_R\\), \\(B_{(1+\\varepsilon)R}-x\\) contains \\(B_{\\varepsilon R}\\). Therefore, uniformly in all point locations,\n\\[\n|Q(\\mu_R,\\nu_R)-N_Ra|\n\\le N_R\\int_{|z|\\ge\\varepsilon R}|f(z)|\\,dz=o(N_R).\n\\]\nNo bound on local point counts is used.</p><p>Furthermore,\n\\[\n\\frac{Q(\\nu_R,\\nu_R)}{V_R}\n=\\int f(z)\\frac{|B_{(1+\\varepsilon)R}\\cap(B_{(1+\\varepsilon)R}-z)|}{V_R}\\,dz\n\\longrightarrow a.\n\\]\nThe overlap fraction is between zero and one and tends to one for fixed \\(z\\), so dominated convergence applies with \\(|f|\\). It is eventually positive. Hence\n\\[\n\\frac{Q(\\mu_R,\\mu_R)}{N_R}\n\\ge\\frac{|Q(\\mu_R,\\nu_R)|^2}{N_RQ(\\nu_R,\\nu_R)}\n=\\frac{N_R}{V_R}\\frac{(a+o(1))^2}{a+o(1)}.\n\\]\nSince \\(N_R/V_R\\to(1+\\varepsilon)^{-2}\\), take the lower limit, then let \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\). Remove the exactly \\(N_R\\) diagonal terms \\(f(0)\\). The assertion for \\(\\Phi\\) follows by termwise comparison of each finite off-diagonal sum.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Sharp Gaussian auxiliaries and Gaussian energy comparison — paper Lemma 6.2 and equations (6.5)–(6.6).** For every \\(\\alpha>0\\), put \\(G_\\alpha(x)=e^{-\\pi\\alpha|x|^2}\\).", "statement": "<p>**Sharp Gaussian auxiliaries and Gaussian energy comparison — paper Lemma 6.2 and equations (6.5)–(6.6).**</p><p>For every \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), put \\(G_\\alpha(x)=e^{-\\pi\\alpha|x|^2}\\). There is a real radial Schwartz function \\(f_\\alpha\\) such that\n\\[\nf_\\alpha\\le G_\\alpha,\\qquad \\widehat f_\\alpha\\ge0,\n\\]\n\\[\nf_\\alpha(a)=G_\\alpha(a)\\quad(a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}),\n\\qquad\n\\widehat f_\\alpha(w)=0\\quad(w\\in A^*\\setminus\\{0\\}).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\widehat f_\\alpha(0)-f_\\alpha(0)\n=\\sum_{a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}}e^{-\\pi\\alpha|a|^2},\n\\]\nand every locally finite configuration \\(C\\) of centered disk density one satisfies\n\\[\n\\liminf_{R\\to\\infty}\\frac1{N_R}\n\\sum_{\\substack{x,y\\in C_R\\\\x\\ne y}}e^{-\\pi\\alpha|x-y|^2}\n\\ge\\sum_{a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}}e^{-\\pi\\alpha|a|^2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(\\alpha\\ge1\\), take\n\\[\nk=\\pi(\\alpha/b-h)\\ge\\pi(2/\\sqrt3-2/5)&gt;2.36.\n\\]\nLet \\(f\\) be supplied by Proposition 5 and put \\(f_\\alpha=ke^{-k}f\\). The multiplier is positive, and, with \\(s=b|x|^2\\),\n\\[\nke^{-k}e^{-\\pi hs}T_k(s)\n=e^{-(\\pi h+k)s}=e^{-\\pi\\alpha|x|^2}.\n\\]\nThe direct and dual shell containment of Proposition 1 gives both contact sets.</p><p>For \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1\\), put \\(\\beta=1/\\alpha&gt;1\\) and define nonnegative radial Schwartz slacks\n\\[\nq_\\beta=G_\\beta-f_\\beta,\\qquad r_\\beta=\\widehat f_\\beta.\n\\]\nFourier transformation twice fixes these radial functions, so\n\\[\nG_\\beta=q_\\beta+\\widehat r_\\beta,\\qquad\n\\beta^{-1}G_\\alpha=\\widehat G_\\beta=\\widehat q_\\beta+r_\\beta.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nf_\\alpha=\\beta\\widehat q_\\beta.\n\\]\nThen \\(G_\\alpha-f_\\alpha=\\beta r_\\beta\\ge0\\) and\n\\(\\widehat f_\\alpha=\\beta q_\\beta\\ge0\\). Since \\(A^*\\) is a rotation of \\(A\\), radiality transfers the known slack zeros to both shell sets, proving the contacts.</p><p>Periodize the fixed Schwartz function \\(f_\\alpha\\) over \\(A\\). Schwartz decay makes the periodization smooth; its Fourier coefficients on the unit-area cell are \\(\\widehat f_\\alpha(w)\\), \\(w\\in A^*\\), and are absolutely summable. Evaluation at zero gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{a\\in A}f_\\alpha(a)=\\sum_{w\\in A^*}\\widehat f_\\alpha(w).\n\\]\nThe contacts and removal of the origin give the sharp lattice identity. Apply Proposition 6 with \\(\\Phi=G_\\alpha\\) to obtain the configuration comparison. Only the lattice was periodized; the competitor need not be periodic.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Positive-mixture transfer — paper Proposition 7.2.** Let \\(C\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) be locally finite with centered disk density one, and assume that for every \\(\\alpha>0\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Positive-mixture transfer — paper Proposition 7.2.**</p><p>Let \\(C\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) be locally finite with centered disk density one, and assume that for every \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\liminf_{R\\to\\infty}\\frac1{N_R}\n\\sum_{\\substack{x,y\\in C_R\\\\x\\ne y}}e^{-\\pi\\alpha|x-y|^2}\n\\ge\\sum_{a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}}e^{-\\pi\\alpha|a|^2}.\n\\]\nThen every smooth completely monotone \\(g:(0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)\\) satisfies\n\\[\nE_g(C)\\ge\\sum_{a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}}g(|a|^2)\n\\]\nin the extended nonnegative reals.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) and put \\(F_\\varepsilon(t)=g(\\varepsilon+t)\\) for \\(t\\ge0\\). Lemma 8.1 gives a finite representing measure \\(\\rho_\\varepsilon\\) of mass \\(g(\\varepsilon)\\).</p><p>Choose any real sequence \\(R_n\\to\\infty\\), discarding finitely many terms so \\(N_{R_n}&gt;0\\), and define\n\\[\nA_n(v)=\\frac1{N_{R_n}}\n\\sum_{\\substack{x,y\\in C_{R_n}\\\\x\\ne y}}v^{|x-y|^2},\n\\qquad\nL(v)=\\sum_{a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}}v^{|a|^2},\n\\quad0\\le v\\le1.\n\\]\nEvery exponent is positive; \\(A_n\\) is continuous and nonnegative, with \\(0^t=0\\) for \\(t&gt;0\\). For \\(0&lt;v&lt;1\\), the Gaussian hypothesis at \\(\\alpha=-\\log v/\\pi\\) gives\n\\[\n\\liminf_nA_n(v)\\ge L(v).\n\\]\nAt zero both sides vanish. At one,\n\\[\nA_n(1)=N_{R_n}-1\\to\\infty=L(1).\n\\]\nThus the endpoint comparison also holds.</p><p>Finite-sum representation, Fatou, and Tonelli give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\liminf_n\\frac1{N_{R_n}}\n\\sum_{x\\ne y}F_\\varepsilon(|x-y|^2)\n&amp;=\\liminf_n\\int A_n\\,d\\rho_\\varepsilon\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\int\\liminf_nA_n\\,d\\rho_\\varepsilon\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\int L\\,d\\rho_\\varepsilon\n=\\sum_{a\\ne0}g(\\varepsilon+|a|^2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll terms are nonnegative, so this remains valid with infinite right side. Since the sequence was arbitrary, the inequality holds for the full-radius lower limit.</p><p>Complete monotonicity gives \\(g'\\le0\\), so termwise \\(g(t)\\ge g(\\varepsilon+t)\\). Hence\n\\[\nE_g(C)\\ge\\sum_{a\\ne0}g(\\varepsilon+|a|^2).\n\\]\nAs \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\), monotone convergence in the lattice sum proves the assertion. No limit in \\(\\varepsilon\\) is exchanged with the configuration lower limit, and no unshifted measure or measurable selection of Gaussian auxiliaries is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Positive mixtures — paper Lemma 7.1.** Let \\(F:[0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)\\) be smooth with one-sided derivatives at zero, and suppose \\( (-1)^jF^{(j)}(t)\\ge0\\qquad(j\\ge0,\\ t\\ge0). \\) There is a finite positive Borel …", "statement": "<p>**Positive mixtures — paper Lemma 7.1.**</p><p>Let \\(F:[0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)\\) be smooth with one-sided derivatives at zero, and suppose\n\\[\n(-1)^jF^{(j)}(t)\\ge0\\qquad(j\\ge0,\\ t\\ge0).\n\\]\nThere is a finite positive Borel measure \\(\\rho\\) on \\([0,1]\\), of total mass \\(F(0)\\), such that\n\\[\nF(t)=\\int_{[0,1]}v^t\\,d\\rho(v)\\qquad(t&gt;0).\n\\]\nMoreover \\(\\rho(\\{0\\})=0\\). An atom at \\(1\\) is allowed and contributes a constant function.</p>", "proof": "<p>First record compactness preserving endpoint masses. For positive measures \\(\\mu_n\\) of bounded mass on \\([0,1]\\), choose a subsequence along which total masses tend to \\(m\\) and \\(\\mu_n([0,q])\\to a(q)\\) for every rational \\(q\\in(0,1)\\). Put\n\\[\nH(x)=\\inf_{\\substack{x&lt;q&lt;1\\\\q\\text{ rational}}}a(q)\n\\quad(0\\le x&lt;1),\\qquad H(1)=m.\n\\]\nThis nondecreasing right-continuous distribution defines a positive measure, including mass \\(H(0)\\) at zero and \\(m-H(1-)\\) at one. Rational bracketing gives convergence at interior continuity points. Step approximations with such endpoints and uniform continuity prove weak convergence against every continuous test function.</p><p>Fix \\(d&gt;0\\), let \\(m_j=F(jd)\\), and write \\(\\Delta m_j=m_{j+1}-m_j\\). Repeated integration gives\n\\[\n(-1)^r\\Delta^rm_j\n=\\int_{[0,d]^r}(-1)^rF^{(r)}(jd+u_1+\\cdots+u_r)\\,du_1\\cdots du_r\\ge0.\n\\]\nDefine \\(\\Lambda(x^j)=m_j\\). For \\(M\\ge1\\), put at \\(p/M\\), \\(0\\le p\\le M\\), mass\n\\[\nw_{M,p}\n=\\Lambda\\!\\left(\\binom Mp x^p(1-x)^{M-p}\\right)\n=\\binom Mp(-1)^{M-p}\\Delta^{M-p}m_p\\ge0.\n\\]\nThe measure \\(\\mu_{M,d}=\\sum_pw_{M,p}\\delta_{p/M}\\) has total mass \\(m_0\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(j\\le M\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{p=0}^M\\frac{\\binom pj}{\\binom Mj}\n\\binom Mp x^p(1-x)^{M-p}=x^j.\n\\]\nApplying \\(\\Lambda\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sum_pw_{M,p}\\frac{\\binom pj}{\\binom Mj}=m_j.\n\\]\nThe polynomials\n\\[\nP_{M,j}(x)=\\prod_{r=0}^{j-1}\\frac{Mx-r}{M-r}\n\\]\nconverge uniformly to \\(x^j\\) on \\([0,1]\\), and at \\(p/M\\) equal the binomial ratio. Thus\n\\[\n\\left|\\int x^j\\,d\\mu_{M,d}-m_j\\right|\n\\le m_0\\|x^j-P_{M,j}\\|_\\infty\\to0.\n\\]\nA weak subsequential limit \\(\\eta_d\\) therefore has all moments\n\\[\nF(jd)=\\int x^j\\,d\\eta_d(x)\\qquad(j=0,1,\\ldots)\n\\]\nand mass \\(F(0)\\).</p><p>For each positive integer \\(\\ell\\), push \\(\\eta_{1/\\ell}\\) forward by \\(x\\mapsto x^\\ell\\), obtaining \\(\\rho_\\ell\\). Then\n\\[\n\\int v^{j/\\ell}\\,d\\rho_\\ell(v)=F(j/\\ell).\n\\]\nChoose a weak subsequence \\(\\rho_{\\ell_n}\\to\\rho\\) before fixing \\(t&gt;0\\). Put \\(t_n=\\lceil\\ell_nt\\rceil/\\ell_n\\). Since\n\\[\n\\sup_{0&lt;v\\le1}v^t|\\log v|=\\frac1{et},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sup_{0\\le v\\le1}|v^{t_n}-v^t|\n\\le\\frac{t_n-t}{et}\\to0.\n\\]\nContinuity of \\(F\\) and weak convergence give the same representation for every \\(t&gt;0\\). Finally \\(v^t\\uparrow\\mathbf1_{(0,1]}(v)\\) as \\(t\\downarrow0\\); monotone convergence and continuity at zero imply\n\\[\n\\rho((0,1])=F(0)=\\rho([0,1]),\n\\]\nso \\(\\rho(\\{0\\})=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The cutoff limit \\(W(E)=\\lim_{\\eta\\downarrow0}W_\\eta(E)\\) exists in \\(\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}\\).", "statement": "<p>**Cutoff control and spatial density — paper Lemma B.2.**</p><p>Use the fixed-\\(s\\), fixed-\\(p\\), unit-background field convention of Theorem 2, but temporarily allow integer multiplicities \\(N_a\\ge1\\):\n\\[\n\\nu=\\sum_aN_a\\delta_a.\n\\]\nSuppress the subscript \\(s\\), writing \\(g,w,\\kappa,f_\\eta,W_\\eta,W\\). Put\n\\[\nQ_\\eta(E,R)=\\int_{K_R\\times\\mathbb R^k}w|E_\\eta|^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nF_\\eta=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}f_\\eta(x,0)\\,dx\n=\\begin{cases}\n\\pi\\eta^2/2,&amp;s=0,\\\\\n\\pi s\\eta^{2-s}/(2-s),&amp;s&gt;0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nFix\n\\[\na_0=\\frac14\\sqrt{2/3},\\qquad a_1=4\\sqrt2,\\qquad\n\\eta_0=\\min(1/8,a_0/16).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The cutoff limit \\(W(E)=\\lim_{\\eta\\downarrow0}W_\\eta(E)\\) exists in \\(\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}\\). There is a constant \\(C_s\\), independent of the field, such that\n\\[\n-C_s\\le W_\\eta(E)\\le W(E)+2\\kappa F_\\eta\n\\qquad(0&lt;\\eta&lt;\\eta_0).\n\\]\nIf \\(Q_\\eta(E,R)=O(R^2)\\) at one fixed cutoff, the same holds at every fixed cutoff and\n\\[\n\\nu(K_R)/R^2\\longrightarrow1.\n\\]\nThe infimum \\(m=\\inf_EW(E)\\) over compatible simple gradients in the prescribed class is a finite real number.</p>", "proof": "<p>The positive unit smearing measure is\n\\[\nd\\delta_0^{(\\eta)}\n=\\kappa^{-1}[-g'(\\eta)]w\\,d\\sigma_{\\partial B_\\eta},\n\\qquad\n-\\operatorname{div}(w\\nabla f_\\eta)=\\kappa(\\delta_0-\\delta_0^{(\\eta)}).\n\\]\nTesting the smeared equation against a nonnegative smooth function equal to one near spheres centered in a fixed unit cell \\(B\\) gives\n\\[\nN_B^2\\le C_{B,\\eta}\n\\left(1+\\int_{B'\\times[-h_0,h_0]^k}w|E_\\eta|^2\\right).\n\\]\nHere \\(B',h_0\\) are fixed enlargements with bounded overlap in a unit-cell tiling. Also, for \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;\\eta\\),\n\\[\n\\int w|\\nabla(f_\\alpha-f_\\eta)|^2\n=\\kappa[g(\\alpha)-g(\\eta)].\n\\]\nApplying the count estimate to the finite-range sum of these differences transfers volume-order energy between any two fixed cutoffs.</p><p>The smeared Gauss equation then gives density one. Choose a lateral boundary within distance one of \\(\\partial K_R\\) with squared flux energy \\(O(R^2)\\). For \\(k=1\\), choose height \\(L\\in[1,\\sqrt R]\\) with top-and-bottom squared energy \\(O(R^{3/2})\\). Weighted Cauchy–Schwarz bounds total flux by\n\\[\nCL^{(\\gamma+1)/2}R^{3/2}\n+CL^{\\gamma/2}R^{7/4}=o(R^2),\n\\]\nsince \\(-1&lt;\\gamma&lt;1\\), using \\(L^{\\gamma/2}\\le1\\) when \\(\\gamma&lt;0\\). Smeared counts bracket counts in squares whose sidelengths differ from \\(R\\) by a fixed constant. Shifted-radius inequalities give full-radius density convergence. For \\(k=0\\), only the lateral estimate is needed.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nS=\\sum_aN_a(f_\\alpha-f_\\eta)(\\cdot-a)\\le0.\n\\]\nFor a horizontal cutoff \\(\\chi\\), integration by parts yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\chi w(|E_\\eta|^2-|E_\\alpha|^2)\n={}&amp;\\kappa\\int\\chi S(\\nu_\\eta+\\nu_\\alpha-2\\delta_{\\mathbb R^2})\\\\\n&amp;-\\int Sw(E_\\alpha+E_\\eta)\\cdot\\nabla\\chi.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nDistinct-charge terms are nonpositive. An interior self term is\n\\[\n-\\kappa[g(\\alpha)-g(\\eta)]N_a^2,\n\\]\nbounded above by the corresponding expression with \\(N_a\\). The background contributes at most \\(2\\kappa F_\\eta\\) per particle. Commutator and transition terms are bounded by fixed-width shell energies and counts, using the local estimate.</p><p>Choose \\(r_j\\) realizing the \\(\\eta\\)-limsup. Average both cutoff shell energies over\n\\([r_j+b_0,r_j+\\sqrt{r_j}]\\), where \\(b_0\\) exceeds twice the shell width. Some \\(t_j\\) has shell cost \\(O(r_j^{3/2})=o(r_j^2)\\). Since \\(t_j/r_j\\to1\\) and \\(Q_\\eta\\) is nonnegative and increasing,\n\\[\n\\frac{Q_\\eta(E,t_j)}{t_j^2}\n\\ge\\frac{r_j^2}{t_j^2}\\frac{Q_\\eta(E,r_j)}{r_j^2}.\n\\]\nThese radii still realize the \\(\\eta\\)-limsup. The count shell is \\(o(t_j^2)\\) by density. Therefore\n\\[\nW_\\eta(E)\\le W_\\alpha(E)+2\\kappa F_\\eta\n\\qquad(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;\\eta&lt;\\eta_0).\n\\]\nComparing with one fixed cutoff proves the uniform lower bound; this almost-monotonicity proves existence of the cutoff limit and the stated upper bound. If every cutoff has infinite energy, the conclusion is immediate. The same argument allows integer multiplicities. A canonical unit-lattice field has finite renormalized value, so \\(-\\infty&lt;m&lt;\\infty\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a horizontally stationary probability law \\(P\\) on compatible simple gradient fields in the fixed-\\(p\\) class, supported on \\(W(E)=m\\).", "statement": "<p>**Stationary minimizers with vanishing close-pair defect — paper Lemma B.3.**</p><p>Retain Proposition 9. Fix a dyadic auxiliary cutoff \\(\\eta'\\in(0,\\eta_0)\\), a decreasing dyadic sequence \\(\\eta_\\ell\\downarrow0\\) with \\(\\eta_1=\\eta'\\), and \\(Q=[-1/2,1/2)^2\\). For distinct locations define\n\\[\nq_\\eta(a-b)\n=f_\\eta(a-b,0)\n+\\int f_\\eta(X-(a,0))\\,d\\delta_b^{(\\eta)}(X),\n\\]\nand, for a simple configuration,\n\\[\n\\mu_\\eta\n=\\kappa\\sum_{a\\in C}\n\\left(\\sum_{b\\in C\\setminus\\{a\\}}q_\\eta(a-b)\\right)\\delta_a.\n\\]\nThe kernel is nonnegative and vanishes for \\(|a-b|\\ge2\\eta\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a horizontally stationary probability law \\(P\\) on compatible simple gradient fields in the fixed-\\(p\\) class, supported on \\(W(E)=m\\). For selected cutoffs, put\n\\[\na_\\eta=\\mathbb E_PW_\\eta(E),\\qquad D_\\eta=\\mathbb E_P\\mu_\\eta(Q).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\na_\\eta\\to m,\\qquad\n0\\le D_\\eta\\le m-a_\\eta+2\\kappa F_\\eta\\to0.\n\\]\nFor each selected \\(\\eta\\), one can choose a realization for which all selected cutoff spatial-energy averages have finite limits,\n\\[\nd_\\eta(E)=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}R^{-2}\\mu_\\eta(K_R)\\le2D_\\eta,\n\\]\nand, when \\(k=1\\), for every fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\ne_{\\varepsilon,R}\n=\\frac1{\\varepsilon^4R^2}\n\\int_{K_R\\times\\{|y|&gt;\\varepsilon^2R/4\\}}w|E|^2\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose simple fields \\(E^j\\) with \\(W(E^j)\\le m+1/j\\). Choose \\(S_j\\), with \\(S_j/j\\to\\infty\\), large enough that upper-limsup estimates hold simultaneously at \\(\\eta_1,\\ldots,\\eta_j\\) on \\(K_{S_j+2j}\\). The enlargement handles every observation window in \\(K_{2j}\\). Let \\(P_j\\) be the law of the translated field and charge measure for a uniform translation in \\(K_{S_j}\\). Tonelli and Proposition 9 give, for\n\\[\n\\Gamma_\\eta(E)=\\int_{Q\\times\\mathbb R^k}w|E_\\eta|^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\limsup_j\\int\\Gamma_{\\eta_\\ell}\\,dP_j\n\\le m+2\\kappa F_{\\eta_\\ell}+\\kappa g(\\eta_\\ell),\n\\]\nand analogous bounds on every fixed base square.</p><p>For compactness use \\(H^{-r}_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\) for fields, \\(r&gt;(2+k)/2+1\\), and vague topology for positive charge measures. Bounded local \\(L^p\\) sets are relatively compact in the first topology. The local count estimate gives second moments of local counts. Weighted Hölder gives unweighted local \\(L^p\\) control of \\(E_\\eta\\) for \\(p&lt;\\min(2,2/s)\\), and singular corrections are locally \\(L^p\\) for \\(p&lt;3/(s+1)\\). For the logarithm both requirements reduce to \\(p&lt;2\\). Markov bounds and a countable exhaustion give tightness, temporarily allowing multiplicities.</p><p>On bounded-count sets, vague convergence identifies each fixed singular correction distributionally, indeed in the permitted local \\(L^p\\) topology. On bounded-energy subsequences, truncated fields have identified weak \\(L^2(w)\\) limits. These pass the smeared equation because test gradients lie in \\(L^2(w)\\). Adding the singular correction recovers the unsmeared equation; weighted Cauchy–Schwarz and locally integrable singular flux give \\(wE\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\). Distributional curl remains zero and local \\(L^p\\) bounds persist. Summable Markov allocations make these statements apply to the limiting law.</p><p>Weighted quadratic energy is lower semicontinuous, as is seen on bounded cylinders from\n\\[\n\\int w|F|^2\n=\\sup_{\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty}\n\\left(2\\langle F,\\psi\\rangle-\\int w^{-1}|\\psi|^2\\right).\n\\]\nTake increasing cylinder heights. A subsequential law \\(P\\) inherits the bounds. It is stationary because translation by fixed \\(z\\) changes an orbit law in total variation by at most\n\\[\n|K_{S_j}\\triangle(K_{S_j}+z)|/S_j^2\\to0.\n\\]</p><p>Apply the \\(L^1\\) pointwise and mean ergodic theorems along centered square Følner sequences in \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\) to each integrable \\(\\Gamma_{\\eta_\\ell}\\). Tiled sums are raw energies on integer squares; positivity and nested squares extend limits to real radii. Limits are conditional expectations on the invariant sigma-algebra, not necessarily constants. Their expectations equal the observable means. Thus\n\\[\na_{\\eta_\\ell}\n=\\mathbb E_P\\Gamma_{\\eta_\\ell}-\\kappa g(\\eta_\\ell)\n\\le m+2\\kappa F_{\\eta_\\ell}.\n\\]\nFatou and the uniform lower bound give \\(\\mathbb E_PW\\le m\\), without yet using simplicity. Hence \\(W\\) is finite and integrable, and Proposition 9 gives dominated convergence \\(a_{\\eta_\\ell}\\to\\mathbb E_PW\\).</p><p>The local count bound gives finite point intensity \\(\\rho\\). For \\(k=0\\), the expected smeared equation and stationarity give \\(\\rho=1\\). For \\(k=1\\), let\n\\[\nb_\\eta(y)=\\mathbb E_P(E_\\eta)_y\n\\]\nand let \\(\\mu_{\\eta,y}\\) be the vertical marginal of one smeared charge. Jensen gives \\(\\int|y|^\\gamma|b_\\eta|^2&lt;\\infty\\), and\n\\[\n-\\partial_y(|y|^\\gamma b_\\eta)\n=\\kappa(\\rho\\mu_{\\eta,y}-\\delta_0).\n\\]\nOutside \\([-\\eta,\\eta]\\), the weighted flux is constant on each tail and must vanish because \\(\\int_1^\\infty y^{-\\gamma}\\,dy=\\infty\\). Integration gives \\(\\rho=1\\).</p><p>For selected \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;\\eta\\), put\n\\[\nd=\\sum_a(f_\\eta-f_\\alpha)(\\cdot-a),\n\\]\ncounting labeled particles. Then\n\\[\nw(|E_\\alpha|^2-|E_\\eta|^2)\n=\\operatorname{div}[wd(E_\\alpha+E_\\eta)]\n+\\kappa d(\\nu_\\alpha+\\nu_\\eta-2\\delta_{\\mathbb R^2}).\n\\]\nPositive cutoffs make \\(d\\) bounded and finite range; second count moments and Cauchy–Schwarz justify expected local products. Test with a horizontal smooth function of integral one and a vertical cutoff constant on \\(|y|\\le\\eta\\). Stationarity annihilates horizontal divergence, and the vertical cutoff varies outside the support. Separating self, distinct-labeled-pair, and background terms gives\n\\[\nD_{\\alpha,\\eta}\n=a_\\alpha-a_\\eta+2\\kappa(F_\\eta-F_\\alpha)\\ge0,\n\\]\nwhere \\(D_{\\alpha,\\eta}\\) is the ordered-pair intensity of\n\\[\n\\kappa\\int(f_\\eta-f_\\alpha)(X-(a,0))\n(d\\delta_b^{(\\alpha)}+d\\delta_b^{(\\eta)})(X).\n\\]\nA coincident labeled pair contributes \\(\\kappa[g(\\alpha)-g(\\eta)]\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\kappa[g(\\alpha)-g(\\eta)]\n\\,\\mathbb E_P\\sum_{a\\in Q}N_a(N_a-1)\n\\le D_{\\alpha,\\eta}.\n\\]\nThe right side stays bounded as \\(\\alpha\\downarrow0\\), while \\(g(\\alpha)\\to\\infty\\). Thus collisions have zero intensity, and a countable tiling gives simplicity almost surely. Only now use \\(W\\ge m\\); hence \\(W=m\\) almost surely and \\(a_\\eta\\to m\\).</p><p>Fatou as \\(\\alpha\\downarrow0\\) yields\n\\[\nD_\\eta\\le m-a_\\eta+2\\kappa F_\\eta\\to0.\n\\]\nThe ergodic theorem gives almost-sure defect density \\(d_\\eta\\) with \\(\\mathbb E_Pd_\\eta=D_\\eta\\). For \\(k=1\\), apply it also to raw energy above each fixed integer height \\(z&gt;1\\). These integrable observables decrease to zero, so their spatial densities do also almost surely. Intersect the countable full-measure events. For each \\(\\eta\\), choose a realization with \\(d_\\eta\\le2D_\\eta\\), or \\(d_\\eta=0\\) if \\(D_\\eta=0\\). Comparing the moving height \\(\\varepsilon^2R/4\\) with each fixed integer height proves \\(e_{\\varepsilon,R}\\to0\\). No rate uniform in \\(\\varepsilon\\) is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Screening without new close pairs — paper Lemma B.4.** Retain the fixed auxiliary cutoff \\(\\eta'\\) and take \\(0<\\eta<\\eta'\\).", "statement": "<p>**Screening without new close pairs — paper Lemma B.4.**</p><p>Retain the fixed auxiliary cutoff \\(\\eta'\\) and take \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;\\eta'\\). Let \\(E\\) be a compatible simple gradient field for which\n\\[\nM_{R,\\rho}=R^{-2}Q_\\rho(E,R)\n\\]\nhas a finite limit for \\(\\rho=\\eta,\\eta'\\). If \\(k=1\\), assume \\(e_{\\varepsilon,R}\\to0\\) for every fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), as defined in Proposition 10; put \\(e_{\\varepsilon,R}=0\\) for \\(k=0\\).</p><p>For every \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;1/8\\) and every sufficiently large integer \\(R\\), there are exactly \\(R^2\\) distinct points \\(\\widehat\\Lambda\\subset K_R\\) and a field \\(\\widehat E\\), not necessarily a gradient, with zero exterior normal flux and\n\\[\n-\\operatorname{div}(w\\widehat E)\n=\\kappa\\left(\\sum_{a\\in\\widehat\\Lambda}\\delta_{(a,0)}\n-\\mathbf1_{K_R}\\delta_{\\mathbb R^2}\\right).\n\\]\nIts truncation has finite weighted energy and\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR^{-2}\\int_{K_R\\times\\mathbb R^k}w|\\widehat E_\\eta|^2\n\\le{}&amp;M_{R,\\eta}\n+C_{s,\\eta'}\\varepsilon(1+g(\\eta))\n(1+M_{R,\\eta}+M_{R,\\eta'})\\\\\n&amp;+o_R(1)+C_se_{\\varepsilon,R}\\varepsilon^{1+\\min(2\\gamma,0)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nEvery pair at distance below \\(2\\eta\\) is a retained original pair in \\(K_R\\), and every point lies at distance at least \\(a_0/2\\) from \\(\\partial K_R\\). Constants are independent of \\(R\\) and the realization; \\(R\\) is chosen after \\(\\eta',\\eta,E,\\varepsilon\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Keep the field and cutoffs fixed. Coarea chooses an inner rectangle \\(K_R'\\), whose faces are between \\(\\varepsilon R\\) and \\(2\\varepsilon R\\) from the outer faces, such that\n\\[\n\\int_{\\partial K_R'\\times\\mathbb R^k}w|E_\\eta|^2\n\\le CM_{R,\\eta}\\varepsilon^{-1}R,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_{(\\partial K_R')_{b_0}\\times\\mathbb R^k}w|E_{\\eta'}|^2\n\\le CM_{R,\\eta'}\\varepsilon^{-1}R,\n\\]\nfor a fixed sufficiently large shell width \\(b_0\\). For \\(k=1\\), choose\n\\[\n\\ell\\in[\\varepsilon^2R/2,\\varepsilon^2R]\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\int_{K_R'\\times\\{\\pm\\ell\\}}w|E|^2\n\\le2\\varepsilon^2Re_{\\varepsilon,R},\n\\]\nby averaging over that interval. For \\(k=0\\), retain \\(\\ell=\\varepsilon^2R\\). Take \\(R\\) large enough that \\(\\ell&gt;2\\sqrt2\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nD_0=K_R'\\times[-\\ell,\\ell]^k,\\qquad\nA_{\\mathrm{sh}}=|K_R|-|K_R'|.\n\\]\nFor \\(k=1\\), let \\(T_\\pm\\) be the old outward weighted flux through the upper and lower faces of \\(D_0\\), and set\n\\[\nC^\\pm=-\\frac{T_\\pm}{A_{\\mathrm{sh}}\\ell^\\gamma}.\n\\]\nThese give separate compatibility in the disconnected upper and lower caps. Omit all such terms for \\(k=0\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\Lambda_0=\\{a\\in C:\\operatorname{dist}(a,\\partial K_R')\\le\\eta\\},\n\\qquad\n\\rho_0=\\sum_{a\\in\\Lambda_0}\\delta_a^{(\\eta)}.\n\\]\nRetain all centers in \\((C\\cap K_R')\\cup\\Lambda_0\\), counted by \\(N_{\\mathrm{old}}\\). All pieces below are finite-energy components of the truncated field; point singularities are restored only at the end.</p><p>Partition the shell into large rectangles \\(H_i\\) with sidelengths in \\([\\ell,2\\ell]\\), using all inner and outer face coordinates as breakpoints. They form a connected annular grid with full shared edges and \\(O_\\varepsilon(1)\\) cells. Independently use a unit-scale tensor grid, sidelengths in \\([1,2]\\), for completing crossing smeared charges.</p><p>The local weighted cell estimates are obtained as follows. For \\(\\Omega=S\\times[-1,1]^k\\), with base sidelengths in \\([1,2]\\), ordinary horizontal Poincaré and trace estimates, integrated against \\(|y|^\\gamma\\), combine with\n\\[\n|v(y)-v(z)|^2\n\\le\\left(\\int_{-1}^1|t|^\\gamma|v'(t)|^2\\,dt\\right)\n\\left(\\int_{-1}^1|t|^{-\\gamma}\\,dt\\right).\n\\]\nBoth weights are integrable. This proves weighted Poincaré, the trace at \\(y=0\\), and lateral traces. Horizontal reflections and short vertical collars, where reflected weights are comparable, followed by a smooth cutoff, yield an extension\n\\[\n\\|\\nabla Tu\\|_{L^2(w)}\\le C_\\gamma\\|\\nabla u\\|_{L^2(w;\\Omega)}\n\\]\nfor the weighted-mean-zero representative.</p><p>Let \\(g_\\eta=g-f_\\eta\\). Local radial integration gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\operatorname{supp}Tu}w|\\nabla g_\\eta(X-(a,0))|^2\n\\le C_s(1+g(\\eta)).\n\\]\nFor \\(s&gt;0\\), the full-space integral is \\(\\kappa g(\\eta)\\); for the logarithm use only a fixed-radius local integral. Positivity of smearing gives\n\\[\n\\left|\\int_\\Omega u\\,d\\delta_a^{(\\eta)}\\right|\n\\le\\int|Tu|\\,d\\delta_a^{(\\eta)}\n=\\kappa^{-1}\\int w\\nabla g_\\eta(\\cdot-a)\\cdot\\nabla|Tu|\n\\le C\\sqrt{1+g(\\eta)}\\,\\|\\nabla u\\|_{L^2(w)}.\n\\]\nFor a cell source \\(\\kappa\\nu\\) of mass \\(Q\\), constant normal data \\(b_F\\) are compatible precisely when\n\\[\nb_F\\int_Fw=-\\kappa Q.\n\\]\nLax–Milgram, using the trace estimates, then gives energy at most \\(CN^2(1+g(\\eta))\\) when at most \\(N\\) labeled charges contribute and summed absolute face flux is at most \\(C\\kappa N\\). Several constant face data with the same total are allowed.</p><p>Construct the shell field as five components.</p><p>**Crossing completion.**\nRestrict \\(\\rho_0\\) to exterior unit cells times \\([-1,1]^k\\). Its support lies within \\(2\\eta\\) of \\(\\partial K_R'\\). Each active cell is a first-layer side cell sharing a full inner face, or a diagonal corner cell. Route each corner through one adjacent side cell. These are trees of uniformly bounded size.</p><p>On each outgoing tree face prescribe integrated outward flux \\(-\\kappa\\) times the total source mass in its subtree; adjacent data are opposite. On a root export the total tree mass through its full inner face. Every cell's total outward flux is minus its own source, hence compatible. Internal fluxes cancel. This gives \\(E_{\\mathrm{cross}}\\), with source \\(\\kappa\\rho_0\\) on the exterior portion, and nonnegative inner outlet density\n\\[\nj=-E_{\\mathrm{cross}}\\cdot\\nu_{\\mathrm{shell}}\n=\\sum_{F\\ \\mathrm{outlet}}\n\\mathbf1_F\\,\\frac{\\kappa Q_F}{\\int_Fw},\n\\qquad \\nu_{\\mathrm{shell}}=-\\nu_0.\n\\]\nDo not cut this field at arbitrary large-cell boundaries. The count estimate and bounded tree overlap give\n\\[\n\\int w|E_{\\mathrm{cross}}|^2\n\\le C(1+g(\\eta))\\sum_{\\mathrm{trees}}N_{\\mathrm{tree}}^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_{\\partial K_R'\\times\\mathbb R^k}wj^2\n\\le C\\sum_{\\mathrm{trees}}N_{\\mathrm{tree}}^2\n\\le C(1+M_{R,\\eta'})\\varepsilon^{-1}R.\n\\]</p><p>**Ideal cell masses and rounding.**\nLet\n\\[\nB_i=\\int_{(\\partial K_R'\\cap\\partial H_i)\\times[-\\ell,\\ell]^k}\nwE_\\eta\\cdot\\nu_0,\n\\qquad\nQ_i=\\int_{(\\partial K_R'\\cap\\partial H_i)\\times[-\\ell,\\ell]^k}wj.\n\\]\nThe latter is the actual outlet flux, not necessarily the restricted source mass in that large cell. Define\n\\[\n\\kappa(m_i-1)|H_i|\n=B_i-\\ell^\\gamma(C^++C^-)|H_i|-Q_i.\n\\]\nGauss' equation, the cap constants, and \\(\\sum_iQ_i=\\kappa\\rho_0(D_0^c)\\) give\n\\[\n\\sum_im_i|H_i|=R^2-N_{\\mathrm{old}}=:N_{\\mathrm{new}}\\in\\mathbb N.\n\\]\nAt fixed parameters, the flux estimates make \\(\\max_i|m_i-1|\\to0\\); take it below \\(1/4\\). For example, the outlet contribution divided by cell area is at most \\(C(1+M_{R,\\eta'})\\varepsilon^{-5}R^{-1}\\).</p><p>Round \\(r_i=m_i|H_i|\\) down and increase the necessary number by one to get integers \\(n_i\\) with\n\\[\n\\sum_in_i=N_{\\mathrm{new}},\\qquad |n_i-r_i|\\le1.\n\\]\nSet \\(\\widetilde m_i=n_i/|H_i|\\in[1/2,3/2]\\). For \\(\\delta_i=n_i-r_i\\), whose sum is zero, choose a rooted spanning tree of the large-cell adjacency graph. On a child-to-parent face prescribe outward flux \\(-\\kappa\\) times the subtree sum of \\(\\delta_i\\), with opposite adjacent flux. The compatible cell source is \\(\\kappa\\delta_i|H_i|^{-1}\\delta_{\\mathbb R^2}\\). These fields form \\(E_{\\mathrm{round}}\\), with zero inner and outer shell flux, and\n\\[\n\\int w|E_{\\mathrm{round}}|^2\n\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nC_{s,\\varepsilon}\\ell^{-s},&amp;s&gt;0,\\\\\nC_\\varepsilon,&amp;s=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThere are \\(O_\\varepsilon(1)\\) cells and bounded tree fluxes. Dilation gives the displayed scaling, so this is \\(o(R^2)\\).</p><p>For large \\(R\\), the rectangular subdivision result used in the paper partitions \\(H_i\\) into \\(n_i\\) rectangles of area \\(1/\\widetilde m_i\\), each sidelength in\n\\[\n[\\tfrac14\\widetilde m_i^{-1/2},\\,4\\widetilde m_i^{-1/2}]\n\\subset[a_0,a_1].\n\\]</p><p>**Vertical and side corrections.**\nFor \\(k=1\\), let \\(E_{\\mathrm{vert}}=\\nabla h_2\\), with\n\\[\nh_2(y)=\\frac{\\ell^\\gamma}{1-\\gamma}\n\\begin{cases}\nC^+y^{1-\\gamma},&amp;y\\ge0,\\\\\nC^-|y|^{1-\\gamma},&amp;y\\le0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIts source is \\(-\\ell^\\gamma(C^++C^-)\\delta_{\\mathbb R^2}\\).</p><p>For \\(H\\) with sidelengths in \\([\\ell,2\\ell]\\), let\n\\(\\Omega=H\\times[-\\ell,\\ell]\\), \\(\\Sigma=\\partial H\\times[-\\ell,\\ell]\\).\nA constant source \\(a\\delta_{\\mathbb R^2}\\) and lateral normal datum \\(b\\), with zero cap data, are compatible when\n\\[\na|H|=-\\int_\\Sigma|y|^\\gamma b.\n\\]\nWeighted Poincaré and trace after dilation give\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega|y|^\\gamma|\\nabla h|^2\n\\le C_\\gamma\\left(a^2\\ell^{3-\\gamma}\n+\\ell\\int_\\Sigma|y|^\\gamma|b|^2\\right)\n\\le C_\\gamma\\ell\\int_\\Sigma|y|^\\gamma|b|^2.\n\\]\nIndeed the traces satisfy\n\\[\n\\int_H|h(x,0)|^2\\le C_\\gamma\\ell^{1-\\gamma}\\int_\\Omega w|\\nabla h|^2,\n\\qquad\n\\int_\\Sigma w|h|^2\\le C_\\gamma\\ell\\int_\\Omega w|\\nabla h|^2;\n\\]\ntesting the equation gives the first estimate, and compatibility gives the second. For \\(k=0\\), ordinary planar Poincaré and trace give the analogous bound.</p><p>For the side field in \\(H_i\\), take\n\\[\na_i=\\kappa(m_i-1)+\\ell^\\gamma(C^++C^-),\n\\qquad b_i=-E_\\eta\\cdot\\nu_0+j\n\\]\non the inner side and zero on other lateral faces. The density equation is exactly compatibility. Summing the cell estimate gives\n\\[\n\\int w|E_{\\mathrm{side}}|^2\n\\le C_{s,\\eta'}\\varepsilon\n(M_{R,\\eta}+1+M_{R,\\eta'})R^2.\n\\]\nThe cap contribution is included in \\(a_i\\); the coarea and outlet bounds supply the factor\n\\(\\ell\\varepsilon^{-1}R\\le\\varepsilon R^2\\).</p><p>**New charges.**\nFor each small rectangle \\(S\\) with barycenter \\(a\\), use on\n\\(\\Omega_S=S\\times(-1,1)^k\\) the \\(\\eta\\)-truncation of the neutral zero-normal-flux Neumann field with source\n\\[\n\\kappa(\\delta_{(a,0)}-\\widetilde m_i\\mathbf1_S\\delta_{\\mathbb R^2}).\n\\]\nExtend its truncated field by zero. The pole is uniformly clear of the boundary. Write its potential as \\(g(\\cdot-a)+v\\), where\n\\[\n-\\operatorname{div}(w\\nabla v)\n=-\\kappa\\widetilde m_i\\mathbf1_S\\delta_{\\mathbb R^2},\n\\qquad\n\\partial_\\nu v=-\\partial_\\nu g(\\cdot-a).\n\\]\nTrace and Poincaré give uniformly bounded correction energy. Testing against capped \\(g_\\eta\\) bounds the cross term by \\(O_s(1)\\), since \\(s&lt;2\\) makes the planar integral finite and boundary clearance controls the boundary terms. Thus each truncated cell costs \\(\\kappa g(\\eta)+O_s(1)\\), and\n\\[\n\\int w|E_{\\mathrm{new}}|^2\n\\le C(1+g(\\eta))N_{\\mathrm{new}},\n\\qquad\nN_{\\mathrm{new}}=O(\\varepsilon R^2)+o(R^2).\n\\]</p><p>The five sources sum to\n\\[\n\\kappa(\\rho_0+\\nu_{\\mathrm{new},\\eta}-\\delta_{\\mathbb R^2}).\n\\]\nOn the inner boundary, crossing and side data sum to\n\\[\n-j+(-E_\\eta\\cdot\\nu_0+j)=-E_\\eta\\cdot\\nu_0.\n\\]\nRounding has paired internal data and zero exterior data. Therefore all interface fluxes cancel. Only in the shell is the squared norm bounded by a fixed factor five; the old field inside \\(D_0\\) is retained with coefficient one. The vertical field costs at most \\(Ce_{\\varepsilon,R}\\varepsilon^3R^2\\).</p><p>**Caps and restoration.**\nSolve upper and lower weighted harmonic caps separately. On the inner horizontal face prescribe\n\\[\n-E_\\eta(x,\\pm\\ell)\\cdot(\\pm e_y)\\quad(x\\in K_R'),\n\\qquad -C^\\pm\\quad(x\\in K_R\\setminus K_R'),\n\\]\nand zero data elsewhere. Their weighted total is\n\\[\n-T_\\pm-A_{\\mathrm{sh}}\\ell^\\gamma C^\\pm=0.\n\\]\nFor an ordinary-mean-zero cap potential,\n\\[\n\\int_{K_R\\times\\{\\ell\\}}w|u|^2\n\\le CR\\max\\{1,(\\ell/R)^\\gamma\\}\\int_{\\mathrm{cap}}w|\\nabla u|^2.\n\\]\nTesting gives cap cost at most\n\\[\nCe_{\\varepsilon,R}\\varepsilon^{1+\\min(2\\gamma,0)}R^2.\n\\]</p><p>Retain \\(E_\\eta\\) in \\(D_0\\), use the five shell fields and two caps, and extend by zero past the exterior square and cap heights. The resulting finite-energy field \\(\\widehat F_\\eta\\) has zero exterior normal flux and source\n\\[\n\\kappa\\left(\\sum_{a\\in\\widehat\\Lambda}\\delta_a^{(\\eta)}\n-\\mathbf1_{K_R}\\delta_{\\mathbb R^2}\\right),\n\\]\nwith exactly \\(R^2\\) points. Define\n\\[\n\\widehat E=\\widehat F_\\eta+\n\\sum_{a\\in\\widehat\\Lambda}\\nabla f_\\eta(\\cdot-(a,0)).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\widehat E_\\eta=\\widehat F_\\eta\\) exactly. The singular corrections lie inside the square, and the fundamental-solution identity gives the unsmeared equation. The energy bounds combine to the proposition.</p><p>Every new barycenter is at least \\(a_0/2\\) from its rectangle faces. Old retained points are inside \\(K_R'\\) or within \\(\\eta\\) of its boundary. Since \\(\\eta&lt;\\eta_0\\le a_0/16\\), new–old distances exceed \\(a_0/2-\\eta&gt;2\\eta\\). Taking \\(R\\) so that \\(\\varepsilon R-\\eta\\ge a_0/2\\) gives outer-boundary clearance and the same clearance at reflected seams. New–new and seam distances exceed \\(2\\eta\\). Thus all remaining close pairs are original retained pairs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Normalized cubic approximation — paper Proposition B.1.** Let \\(\\mathcal A_1\\) be the compatible gradients in the fixed-\\(p\\) class of Theorem 2, with simple locally finite configurations and unit background.", "statement": "<p>**Normalized cubic approximation — paper Proposition B.1.**</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal A_1\\) be the compatible gradients in the fixed-\\(p\\) class of Theorem 2, with simple locally finite configurations and unit background. No centered-density hypothesis is added. Define \\(W\\) with the square upper limit at fixed cutoff followed by cutoff removal, as in Theorem 2.</p><p>Then \\(W(E)\\in\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}\\), it is uniformly bounded below on \\(\\mathcal A_1\\), and\n\\[\nm=\\inf_{E\\in\\mathcal A_1}W(E)\n\\]\nis finite. There are integers \\(R_j\\to\\infty\\) and simple configurations \\(C_j\\), periodic under \\(2R_j\\mathbb Z^2\\), with \\(4R_j^2\\) points per cell, whose canonical periodic values satisfy\n\\[\nV(C_j)\\longrightarrow m.\n\\]\nThe approximating fields can be canonical periodic gradients in the same fixed-\\(p\\) class.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 9 proves the cutoff limit, uniform lower bound, and finiteness of \\(m\\). Choose a selected dyadic cutoff \\(\\eta\\) and a realization from Proposition 10, then apply Proposition 11 after choosing \\(\\varepsilon\\), and finally a sufficiently large integer \\(R\\).</p><p>Reflect the screened square in both coordinate directions and repeat with period \\(2R\\mathbb Z^2\\). Zero normal flux gives no interface charges. The cell has \\(4R^2\\) points and area \\(4R^2\\). Reflection multiplies energy and defect by four. No new close pairs occur, so the resulting defect per area is at most the old anchored defect in \\(K_R\\), divided by \\(R^2\\), tending to at most \\(d_\\eta(E)\\le2D_\\eta\\).</p><p>Let \\(E^{\\mathrm{per}}\\) be this field and \\(E^{\\mathrm{can}}\\) the canonical periodic gradient for the same charges. At fixed cutoff,\n\\[\nD=E^{\\mathrm{per}}-E^{\\mathrm{can}}\n=E_\\eta^{\\mathrm{per}}-E_\\eta^{\\mathrm{can}}\n\\]\nis in \\(L^2(w)\\) on the full period cylinder and has zero weighted divergence. Testing against the canonical truncated potential, with vertical cutoffs tending to infinity, makes the cross term vanish: nonconstant Fourier modes decay vertically and the zero-mode derivative is compactly supported. Therefore\n\\[\n\\int w|E_\\eta^{\\mathrm{per}}|^2\n=\\int w|E_\\eta^{\\mathrm{can}}|^2+\\int w|D|^2.\n\\]\nProjection decreases cutoff energy and preserves the defect.</p><p>The canonical field belongs to the prescribed class. Weighted Hölder gives unweighted local \\(L^p\\) for \\(p&lt;\\min(2,2/s)\\), and singular corrections are locally \\(L^p\\) for \\(p&lt;3/(s+1)\\). For \\(s=0\\), use \\(p&lt;2\\). Weighted Cauchy–Schwarz and singular flux integrability give \\(wE^{\\mathrm{can}}\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\).</p><p>For a finite simple periodic configuration, integrate the cutoff-difference identity used in Proposition 10 over a cell and let the smaller cutoff tend to zero. This gives the exact periodic defect identity\n\\[\nV(C)-W_\\eta(E_C^{\\mathrm{can}})\n=\\frac{\\kappa}{|T|}\n\\sum_{a\\in T}\\sum_{b\\ne a}q_\\eta(a-b)-2\\kappa F_\\eta.\n\\]\nThe second sum includes all periodic images, omitting only the coincident self particle.</p><p>Keep \\(\\eta'\\) fixed. For each selected \\(\\eta\\), choose its realization, then \\(\\varepsilon\\) so the middle screening error is arbitrarily small, then an arbitrarily large integer \\(R\\) so all energy and defect limits, moving-tail estimates, size requirements, and seam clearances hold. Subtracting \\(\\kappa g(\\eta)\\), projecting, and using the defect identity gives\n\\[\nV(C_{\\eta,R})\n\\le m+2\\kappa F_\\eta+o(1)+2D_\\eta-2\\kappa F_\\eta\n=m+2D_\\eta+o(1).\n\\]\nSince \\(D_\\eta\\to0\\), a diagonal choice gives the cubic sequence. Every canonical approximant belongs to \\(\\mathcal A_1\\), so its value is also at least \\(m\\). All box limits precede cutoff removal; the limits have not been interchanged.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Heat comparison across period lattices — paper Lemma 8.2.** Let \\(0\\le s<2\\), and let \\(C,D\\) be simple periodic configurations of density one.", "statement": "<p>**Heat comparison across period lattices — paper Lemma 8.2.**</p><p>Let \\(0\\le s&lt;2\\), and let \\(C,D\\) be simple periodic configurations of density one. For\n\\[\nC=\\bigcup_{i=1}^N(a_i+L),\\qquad |\\mathbb R^2/L|=N,\n\\]\nlet \\(V_s(C)=W_{s,L}(a_1,\\ldots,a_N)\\), with the canonical field normalization of Theorem 2. Define\n\\[\nq_0=2\\pi,\\qquad\nq_s=\\frac{2^{2-s}\\pi\\Gamma(1-s/2)}{\\Gamma(s/2)}\\quad(s&gt;0),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Psi_t(x)=(4\\pi t)^{-1}e^{-|x|^2/(4t)},\\qquad\nP_{t,L}(x)=\\sum_{v\\in L}\\Psi_t(x-v),\n\\]\n\\[\nH_C(t)=\\frac1N\\sum_{i,j=1}^NP_{t,L}(a_i-a_j)-\\Psi_t(0).\n\\]\nDefine \\(H_D\\) with its own period lattice and point count.</p><p>Then the following integral is absolutely convergent:\n\\[\nV_s(C)-V_s(D)\n=\\frac{\\kappa_sq_s}{\\Gamma(1-s/2)}\n\\int_0^\\infty(H_C(t)-H_D(t))t^{-s/2}\\,dt.\n\\]\nThe configurations need not share a period lattice.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\alpha=1-s/2\\). On the base plane,\n\\[\n(-\\Delta)^\\alpha g_s(\\cdot,0)=q_s\\delta_0.\n\\]\nFor \\(T=\\mathbb R^2/L\\), its mean-zero unit-source scalar Green function \\(G_L\\) has coefficient\n\\[\nN^{-1}\\lambda_\\xi^{-\\alpha},\\qquad\n\\lambda_\\xi=(2\\pi|\\xi|)^2,\\qquad \\xi\\in L^*\\setminus\\{0\\}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(s&gt;0\\), the even profile\n\\[\nu_\\lambda(y)=\\frac1{\\Gamma(\\alpha)}\n\\int_0^\\infty t^{\\alpha-1}e^{-\\lambda t-y^2/(4t)}\\,dt\n\\]\ndecays at infinity and satisfies\n\\[\nu_\\lambda(0)=\\lambda^{-\\alpha},\\qquad\nu_\\lambda''+\\frac{1-2\\alpha}{y}u_\\lambda'-\\lambda u_\\lambda=0\n\\quad(y&gt;0).\n\\]\nDifferentiation and \\(v=y^2/(4t)\\) give the full two-sided weighted flux\n\\[\n-2\\lim_{y\\downarrow0}y^{1-2\\alpha}u_\\lambda'(y)\n=2^{2-2\\alpha}\\frac{\\Gamma(1-\\alpha)}{\\Gamma(\\alpha)}\n=\\frac{\\kappa_s}{q_s}.\n\\]\nThus the unit-source extension mode is\n\\((q_s/(N\\kappa_s))u_{\\lambda_\\xi}\\). The zero mode is constant by finite tail energy and is zero by mean-zero trace. Therefore the extension Green trace \\(\\mathcal G_L\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_L(x,0)=\\frac{q_s}{\\kappa_s}G_L(x).\n\\]\nFor \\(s=0\\), both solve the same mean-zero Laplace equation and \\(q_0/\\kappa_0=1\\).</p><p>The periodic integration-by-parts identity, with the specified full-space normalization, is\n\\[\nW_{s,L}\n=\\kappa_s^2\\left[\n\\frac1N\\sum_{i\\ne j}\\mathcal G_L(a_i-a_j,0)\n+\\lim_{x\\to0}\\left(\\mathcal G_L(x,0)-\\frac{g_s(x)}{\\kappa_s}\\right)\n\\right].\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nW_{s,L}\n=\\kappa_sq_s\\left[\n\\frac1N\\sum_{i\\ne j}G_L(a_i-a_j)\n+\\lim_{x\\to0}\\left(G_L(x)-\\frac{g_s(x)}{q_s}\\right)\n\\right].\n\\]</p><p>The scalar heat representation is\n\\[\nG_L(x)=\\frac1{\\Gamma(\\alpha)}\n\\int_0^\\infty(P_{t,L}(x)-N^{-1})t^{\\alpha-1}\\,dt.\n\\]\nIts spectral proof applies to every flat torus. Define\n\\[\nB_C(t,x)=\\frac1N\\sum_{i,j}P_{t,L}(a_i-a_j+x),\n\\qquad\nS_C(x)=\\frac1N\\sum_{i,j}G_L(a_i-a_j+x).\n\\]\nThen \\(B_C(t,0)=H_C(t)+\\Psi_t(0)\\). Both \\(S_C,S_D\\) have singular part \\(g_s(x)/q_s\\). In their heat difference, backgrounds are both one and diagonal heat terms are both \\(\\Psi_t(x)\\), so both cancel.</p><p>Uniformly for \\(x\\) near zero, all other image distances are positive; the remaining difference is bounded by \\(Ct^{-1}e^{-c/t}\\) as \\(t\\downarrow0\\). At infinity the two torus spectral gaps give \\(Ce^{-\\lambda t}\\). Dominated convergence as \\(x\\to0\\) therefore gives the claimed absolutely convergent formula. At \\(s=0\\), the logarithmic singularity cancels before either endpoint is integrated.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 7, "cite"], [2, 12, "cite"], [2, 13, "cite"], [2, 14, "cite"], [2, 16, "cite"], [2, 18, "cite"], [2, 22, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [3, 12, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [6, 2, "cite"], [7, 13, "cite"], [7, 14, "cite"], [7, 15, "cite"], [7, 17, "cite"], [7, 18, "cite"], [7, 20, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 4, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [9, 14, "cite"], [9, 18, "cite"], [10, 7, "cite"], [11, 5, "cite"], [11, 7, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [12, 14, "cite"], [12, 15, "cite"], [12, 17, "cite"], [12, 18, "cite"], [12, 20, "cite"], [13, 16, "cite"], [13, 18, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 22, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [18, 16, "cite"], [18, 20, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 16, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 0, "cite"], [22, 1, "cite"], [23, 0, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [25, 28, "cite"], [26, 27, "cite"], [26, 28, "cite"], [27, 28, "cite"], [28, 1, "cite"], [29, 1, "cite"]]});
